/// DataArchitectQuestionBank — 120 questions (102 single-select, 18 multi-select)
import Foundation

enum DataArchitectQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A retail company's architect must link Order Line Items to Products in a model expecting 2 million line items per year. The child records must be deleted automatically when the parent Order is deleted, and rollup totals are required on the Order. Which relationship type should be recommended?", options: [("A", "Lookup relationship from Order Line Item to Order with an Apex trigger to cascade deletes and a scheduled batch job to recalculate totals nightly."), ("B", "Master-detail relationship from Order Line Item to Order, since it cascades deletes and enables roll-up summary fields for order totals."), ("C", "Master-detail relationship from Order to Order Line Item, since the detail object should always be the one with higher volume."), ("D", "Hierarchical relationship from Order Line Item to Order, since hierarchical relationships support cascade delete on custom objects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Master-detail relationships enforce cascade delete and are the only relationship type that supports native roll-up summary fields, matching both stated requirements directly. A is incorrect: Lookup relationships require custom code to replicate cascade delete and rollups, adding maintenance risk the platform already solves natively. C is incorrect: Master-detail direction is fixed by which object is the \"detail\" (child); it is not chosen based on volume. D is incorrect: Hierarchical relationships are only supported on the User object and cannot be used between custom objects.,")
        Question(id: "2", question: "An architect is comparing denormalized versus normalized designs for a claims-processing org where adjusters frequently run reports joining claim, policy, and customer data. Which consideration should most influence the decision?", options: [("A", "Normalized models always outperform denormalized ones because they reduce total record count across the org."), ("B", "Denormalization is irrelevant in Salesforce because SOQL does not support joins of any kind between custom objects."), ("C", "A more denormalized model with fields copied via formulas or workflow reduces cross-object joins in SOQL and reporting, at the cost of some data duplication and sync complexity."), ("D", "Normalized models eliminate the need for indexes since Salesforce automatically indexes every relationship field for join performance.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "SOQL relationship queries and cross-object reports carry governor-limit and performance costs; denormalizing frequently-reported fields onto the primary object reduces query complexity at the acceptable cost of duplicated data. A is incorrect: Record count is not the dominant performance factor; query pattern and index selectivity matter more than raw row counts. B is incorrect: SOQL does support parent-to-child and child-to-parent relationship queries, so this claim is factually wrong. D is incorrect: Lookup and master-detail fields are indexed, but that doesn't remove the cost of traversing multiple relationships in a single query.,")
        Question(id: "3", question: "What is the maximum number of master-detail relationships allowed on a single custom object, and how does this constrain a complex domain model?", options: [("A", "An object can have unlimited master-detail relationships, so hierarchy depth is never a design constraint."), ("B", "An object supports up to five master-detail relationships, matching the limit for lookup relationships on the same object."), ("C", "An object supports up to two master-detail relationships, enabling three-way junction objects but forcing deeper hierarchies to use lookups instead."), ("D", "Only one master-detail relationship is permitted per object, meaning junction objects must always use two lookups instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Salesforce allows a maximum of two master-detail relationships per object, which is exactly enough to build a three-way junction object but requires falling back to lookups for additional parents. A is incorrect: There is a hard platform limit of two, so unlimited relationships is factually incorrect. B is incorrect: Five is the lookup relationship limit context conflated incorrectly with master-detail, which caps at two. D is incorrect: One master-detail is not the actual limit; the correct cap of two is what enables three-way junctions.,")
        Question(id: "4", question: "What is the maximum number of lookup relationships permitted on a single custom object, and why does this matter in a complex domain model?", options: [("A", "Up to 25 lookup relationships per object, identical to the limit on custom fields of any type combined."), ("B", "Up to 40 lookup relationships per object, which is rarely a binding constraint but should still be tracked as the model grows to avoid hitting the ceiling on highly connected hub objects."), ("C", "Unlimited lookup relationships, since only master-detail relationships are capped by the platform."), ("D", "Up to 10 lookup relationships per object, requiring most hub-and-spoke models to introduce junction objects almost immediately.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The platform limit is 40 lookup relationships per object; for most models this is generous, but architects modeling a highly connected \"hub\" object must still track it as volume of relationships grows. A is incorrect: 25 conflates an unrelated Salesforce limit and is not the actual lookup relationship cap. C is incorrect: Lookup relationships are capped just like master-detail, so \"unlimited\" is incorrect. D is incorrect: 10 is too low and would force unnecessary junction objects long before the real ceiling is reached.,")
        Question(id: "5", question: "A finance object rolls up child transaction amounts to a parent Account using a roll-up summary field. The business now needs the same aggregation, but the parent-child relationship is a standard lookup, not master-detail. Which approaches correctly achieve the aggregate on Account? (Choose 3\\)", options: [("A", "Use a scheduled or record-triggered Flow that recalculates and updates the aggregate field on the Account after relevant transaction changes."), ("B", "Create a formula field on the Account that performs the aggregation across the lookup relationship using a cross-object formula."), ("C", "Use a public group and sharing rule to expose the child records so a roll-up summary field can reference them across the lookup."), ("D", "Convert the lookup to master-detail (if ownership and deletion behavior allow it) and use a native roll-up summary field, since that unlocks the declarative aggregation Salesforce provides across master-detail."), ("E", "Use Apex triggers on the child object to calculate and write the aggregate value to a field on the Account whenever a transaction is inserted, updated, or deleted.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3, 4], explanation: "Apex triggers and Flow-based automation are the standard declarative/programmatic ways to aggregate across a lookup relationship where native roll-up summary fields don't apply; converting to master-detail (where feasible) is the third valid path since it unlocks native roll-up summary support directly. B is incorrect: Formula fields cannot perform aggregate functions like SUM or COUNT across a one-to-many lookup relationship. C is incorrect: Sharing rules control record visibility, not aggregation capability, and have no effect on roll-up summary field availability across a lookup.,")
        Question(id: "6", question: "In a high-volume manufacturing org, a single Account has over 200,000 related child Work Order records connected by master-detail. Which behavior of roll-up summary fields should the architect flag as a risk?", options: [("A", "Roll-up summary fields require a custom Apex batch class to be manually invoked, since Salesforce does not compute them automatically."), ("B", "Roll-up summary fields only recalculate once nightly via a scheduled Salesforce job, so real-time totals will always be stale by up to 24 hours."), ("C", "Roll-up summary fields are automatically disabled once a parent exceeds 50,000 child records to protect governor limits."), ("D", "Roll-up summary fields recalculate synchronously on every child DML, so mass updates to Work Orders under one Account can trigger recalculation contention and slow transactions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Roll-up summary recalculation is synchronous with DML on detail records, so under heavy concurrent updates to a large child set this becomes a recognized source of lock contention and slower transactions, informing the data skew design. A is incorrect: Roll-up summary fields are declarative and calculated automatically by the platform; no manual Apex invocation is required. B is incorrect: Recalculation is synchronous/near-real-time on relevant DML, not a nightly batch process. C is incorrect: There is no such automatic disablement threshold on roll-up summary fields at 50,000 children.,")
        Question(id: "7", question: "A team wants configuration-driven behavior (e.g., feature toggles, regional tax rates) that changes rarely and should be deployable across environments without impacting record storage limits. Formula fields alone cannot hold static configuration values that differ per record type. What should be recommended over Custom Settings?", options: [("A", "Standard picklist fields with global value sets, since these are also deployable as metadata and support relationships."), ("B", "Formula fields alone, since they can store any configuration value type without needing separate records at all."), ("C", "Big Objects, since they are optimized for storing high volumes of rarely-changing configuration values."), ("D", "Custom Metadata Types, since they are deployable as metadata, support relationships to other metadata, and don't consume data storage the way records in custom objects would.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Custom Metadata Types are treated as metadata (deployable via change sets/packages, not consuming data storage) and support relationship fields to other metadata, making them ideal for configuration-driven behavior at scale. A is incorrect: Global value sets store picklist options, not structured multi-field configuration records with relationships. B is incorrect: Formula fields compute from existing data; they cannot independently store standalone configuration values like tax rates per record type. C is incorrect: Big Objects are designed for large historical/archival data volumes, not small sets of configuration values, and lack the metadata deployability custom metadata types offer.,")
        Question(id: "8", question: "How does choosing custom metadata types over hardcoded formula-field logic affect governor limits at scale in an automation-heavy org?", options: [("A", "Custom metadata type records can be queried via SOQL without counting against the same query row limits as regular sObjects, offering unlimited query volume."), ("B", "Custom metadata queries count against standard SOQL governor limits, but centralizing logic in metadata avoids duplicating formula logic across many fields and reduces the automation footprint that consumes limits."), ("C", "Custom metadata types eliminate governor limits entirely for any Apex or Flow that references them, since they are treated as read-only static resources."), ("D", "Formula fields never count against governor limits, making them always superior to custom metadata types for any configuration logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Custom metadata type queries still consume standard SOQL limits, but the architectural benefit is reducing redundant formula/automation logic spread across many fields, which lowers the overall automation and query footprint at scale. A is incorrect: Custom metadata SOQL queries are still subject to standard per-transaction query limits; there is no special unlimited exemption. C is incorrect: There is no governor limit exemption granted simply because a metadata type is queried; normal limits still apply. D is incorrect: Formula fields do count indirectly toward limits (e.g., via triggering more complex query plans or view state), so this blanket claim is false.,")
        Question(id: "9", question: "An architect is deciding between a dedicated junction object and multiple lookup fields on one object to represent a many-to-many relationship between Students and Courses. What is the key implication for cascade delete behavior?", options: [("A", "A junction object built with two master-detail relationships cascades deletes from either parent, automatically removing junction records when a Student or Course is deleted."), ("B", "Multiple lookup fields on one object provide the same automatic cascade delete behavior as a junction object, just without the extra object."), ("C", "Neither approach supports cascade delete; both require a nightly batch Apex job to clean up orphaned records."), ("D", "A junction object only cascades deletes from one designated master, while the other relationship must always be a lookup to avoid accidental deletion.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A true junction object uses two master-detail relationships, so deleting either parent (Student or Course) automatically cascades and deletes the junction record, keeping the many-to-many association clean without custom cleanup code. B is incorrect: Lookup relationships do not cascade delete; deleting a Student or Course would leave orphaned lookup references requiring manual cleanup. C is incorrect: Master-detail-based junction objects do cascade delete natively; no batch job is required for that behavior. D is incorrect: A standard junction object design uses two master-detail relationships, not one master-detail and one lookup, specifically to get cascade delete from both sides.,")
        Question(id: "10", question: "Continuing the Student/Course scenario, what is the sharing model implication of using a junction object with two master-detail relationships versus two lookup fields?", options: [("A", "Master-detail junction objects cannot have their own sharing rules at all, unlike lookup-based designs."), ("B", "The junction object always uses its own independent Private OWD regardless of the parent relationship types."), ("C", "Lookup-based designs automatically grant the same implicit sharing as master-detail, so the choice has no sharing impact."), ("D", "The junction object inherits sharing implicitly from both parent master-detail relationships, so a user needs access to both the Student and Course to see the junction record.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "With two master-detail relationships, the junction object's access is controlled by inherited sharing from both parents (a user must have access through both relationships), which is the defining sharing trade-off versus a lookup-based design with independent OWD. A is incorrect: Master-detail-based objects can still have additional sharing rules layered for edge cases; this blanket restriction is incorrect. B is incorrect: Master-detail child objects cannot have an independent OWD; they inherit sharing from the parent(s), which directly contradicts this option. C is incorrect: Lookup relationships do not grant implicit access; the junction object would need its own OWD and possibly sharing rules, which is a materially different model.,")
        Question(id: "11", question: "A support system uses the WhatId field on Task to relate activities to Accounts, Opportunities, or Cases interchangeably. An architect is asked which characteristics of this polymorphic lookup pattern are accurate when deciding whether to keep it versus adding dedicated lookup fields per object type. (Choose 3\\)", options: [("A", "Querying WhatId's target-specific fields in SOQL requires the TYPEOF construct to conditionally select fields based on the referenced object type."), ("B", "Dedicated lookup fields to each object type always outperform WhatId in SOQL because polymorphic fields cannot be indexed at all."), ("C", "WhatId cannot be referenced in any reporting or activity timeline component, making dedicated lookups the only viable option."), ("D", "WhatId has real list view and filtering restrictions compared to a standard, single-target lookup field, which should be weighed against the consolidation benefit."), ("E", "WhatId consolidates related-list display and activity timeline features across multiple object types without requiring separate fields and duplicate automation per related object.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3, 4], explanation: "WhatId's core value is unifying activity relating across object types (E), but that convenience comes with real query complexity (needing TYPEOF for conditional field selection, B) and list view/filtering limitations (D) that must be weighed against dedicated per-object lookups. B is incorrect: Polymorphic lookup fields like WhatId are indexed by Salesforce; claiming they cannot be indexed at all is factually incorrect. C is incorrect: WhatId is explicitly supported by the standard activity timeline and reporting; this claim that it cannot be referenced at all is incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "12", question: "A B2C subscription business sells directly to individual consumers with no company affiliation for most customers. Which data model should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Person Accounts, since they merge Account and Contact into a single object appropriate for individual consumers without meaningful business account structure."), ("B", "Standard Business Accounts with a single default Contact per Account, since this avoids any licensing or reporting complexity."), ("C", "A custom \"Consumer\" object entirely separate from Account and Contact to avoid platform license restrictions."), ("D", "Standard Business Accounts only, using the Account Name field to store the individual's full name.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Person Accounts are purpose-built for B2C models where most customers are individuals rather than businesses, combining Account and Contact fields into a single record and simplifying both data entry and reporting for that use case. B is incorrect: Forcing a Business Account/Contact pair per individual adds unnecessary object overhead and complicates automation compared to the native Person Account model. C is incorrect: A fully custom object forfeits native integration with standard Sales/Service Cloud features (activities, opportunities, cases) that Person Accounts retain. D is incorrect: Storing a person's name in the Account Name field breaks standard Contact-based functionality like activity relating and loses first/last name structure.,")
        Question(id: "13", question: "What trade-off should an architect flag when a customer decides to enable Person Accounts partway through an existing implementation?", options: [("A", "Enabling Person Accounts is fully reversible at any time with no impact on existing integrations, reports, or record types."), ("B", "Person Accounts only affects the mobile app UI and has no bearing on integrations, APIs, or reporting structure."), ("C", "Person Accounts is a one-way, non-reversible org setting, and pre-existing integrations or reports built purely around Business Account/Contact assumptions may need rework to handle the merged record type correctly."), ("D", "Person Accounts can be enabled and disabled per profile, so only some users are affected while others keep the standard model.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Enabling Person Accounts is an irreversible, org-wide change that introduces a special Account record type; any integration, report, or API logic built assuming a strict Account/Contact separation must be reviewed and often reworked. A is incorrect: Person Accounts cannot be disabled once enabled, making this a critical, permanent design decision rather than a reversible toggle. B is incorrect: The change affects API payloads, record type logic, and reporting structure across the org, not just UI presentation. D is incorrect: Person Accounts is an org-wide feature; it cannot be selectively enabled per profile.,")
        Question(id: "14", question: "What constraint should an architect consider when designing an External ID field strategy for integration with three external systems that each provide their own unique identifier for the same Account?", options: [("A", "External ID fields must always be of type Text and cannot be Number, preventing numeric identifiers from external systems from being used directly."), ("B", "Any number of fields can be flagged as External ID, and the platform automatically merges records that share any matching external ID value across sources."), ("C", "Salesforce allows only one field per object to be marked as an External ID, so an architect must combine external identifiers into a single composite field or use several distinct fields, only one of which can carry the \"External ID\" attribute at a time for upsert-by-key behavior with a given source."), ("D", "External ID fields are automatically indexed only if they are also marked Unique, and indexing has no effect on upsert performance otherwise.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "While multiple fields can technically be marked External ID, only one is typically used for a given upsert operation's key; when integrating three systems each with a different identifier, the architect needs distinct fields per source and clear rules for which one drives upserts for each integration. A is incorrect: External ID fields support Text, Number, and Email field types, so restricting to Text only is inaccurate. B is incorrect: There is no automatic cross-source merge behavior triggered simply by matching External ID values; merging still requires explicit logic or the standard merge process. D is incorrect: External ID fields are indexed by the platform regardless of the Unique flag, which is precisely what makes them efficient for upsert lookups.,")
        Question(id: "15", question: "How do External ID field constraints shape an integration architect's identity strategy when three legacy systems must all write into the same Salesforce Contact object?", options: [("A", "Since only one External ID field is allowed org-wide across all objects, one of the three systems must be redesigned to stop sending its own key."), ("B", "Because each integration needs a reliable idempotent upsert key, the architect should create separate indexed External ID fields per source system rather than trying to force a single shared identifier across systems with different key formats."), ("C", "External ID fields cannot be referenced in Bulk API upsert calls, so REST callouts must be used exclusively for identity-based updates."), ("D", "External ID constraints require that all source identifiers be converted to Salesforce Record IDs before any integration can run.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Supporting multiple independent upsert-capable identity keys is exactly what per-source, indexed External ID fields are designed for, letting each integration reliably upsert using its own native key format without collisions. A is incorrect: The External ID limit is per-object (multiple fields allowed), not a single org-wide field, so no source system needs to be redesigned for this reason. C is incorrect: Bulk API fully supports upsert against External ID fields; this is one of its primary supported use cases. D is incorrect: Requiring pre-conversion to Salesforce Record IDs defeats the purpose of External ID-based upsert, which exists specifically to avoid needing the Salesforce ID upfront.,")
        Question(id: "16", question: "A data model uses the standard compound Address field on Account instead of separate custom text fields for street, city, state, and postal code. What should the architect flag as a trade-off?", options: [("A", "Compound Address fields are always faster to migrate via Data Loader because the entire address loads as a single CSV column."), ("B", "Compound fields like Address cannot be individually filtered in SOQL WHERE clauses on their sub-fields, and geolocation-based compound fields have specific distance-function query support, both of which differ from plain custom text fields."), ("C", "Compound fields cannot appear on page layouts, so they must be split into individual custom fields before use."), ("D", "Compound Address fields support roll-up summary aggregation directly, unlike individual custom text fields.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Sub-fields of compound fields like Address (Street, City, etc.) can actually be queried individually in SOQL, but the compound Geolocation field specifically enables specialized distance queries not available with plain text/number fields, and this special query behavior is the key trade-off architects must account for versus custom field alternatives. A is incorrect: Data Loader migration for compound fields actually requires mapping to the individual sub-fields (e.g., MailingStreet, MailingCity), not a single combined column, making this claim incorrect. C is incorrect: Compound fields do appear on page layouts as a single element composed of their sub-fields; splitting is not required for layout support. D is incorrect: Roll-up summary fields aggregate numeric/date/currency child fields; Address is not a supported source field type for roll-up summaries.,")
        Question(id: "17", question: "How does implicit sharing through a master-detail relationship differ architecturally from explicit sharing rules for a child object holding sensitive claim documents?", options: [("A", "Master-detail implicit sharing ties child record visibility directly to the parent's access level and cannot be overridden by separate sharing rules on the child, whereas explicit sharing rules on a lookup-based child object can grant independent, more granular access regardless of parent visibility."), ("B", "Sharing rules always take precedence over master-detail implicit sharing, so sensitive child data can be hidden from users who can see the parent."), ("C", "Implicit sharing via master-detail only applies to Contacts and Opportunities, not to arbitrary custom child objects."), ("D", "Explicit sharing rules cannot be created on any object that participates in a master-detail relationship.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Master-detail child records cannot have independent OWD or sharing rules — access always mirrors the parent — so if sensitive claim documents need finer-grained, independent access control, a lookup relationship with its own sharing rules is architecturally required instead. B is incorrect: Sharing rules cannot override master-detail implicit inheritance; a user with parent access always sees the child, which is the core limitation being tested. C is incorrect: Master-detail implicit sharing applies to any custom master-detail child object, not just Contacts/Opportunities. D is incorrect: Sharing rules can still be created on master-detail child objects for supplemental grants, but they cannot restrict below what implicit sharing already grants.,")
        Question(id: "18", question: "What architectural implication follows from using master-detail (rather than a lookup with sharing rules) for a child object containing sensitive HR case notes?", options: [("A", "Master-detail relationships allow field-level security to fully suppress visibility of sensitive child records even when the parent is visible."), ("B", "Any user granted access to the parent record automatically sees the sensitive child notes, so if segregation of duties requires hiding notes from some users who can see the parent, master-detail is the wrong relationship choice."), ("C", "Master-detail child objects support their own OWD setting of Private specifically to protect sensitive data, independent of the parent."), ("D", "Sensitive child data under master-detail is automatically encrypted at rest, unlike data under a lookup relationship.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Because master-detail forces the child's visibility to mirror the parent's, any user who can see the parent case can see the sensitive notes; this is precisely the scenario where independent, more restrictive access requires a lookup relationship with dedicated sharing rules instead. A is incorrect: Field-level security controls field visibility for users who already have record access, but it cannot suppress an entire child record's existence when a user already has parent-inherited access. C is incorrect: Master-detail children cannot set an independent OWD; they always inherit from the parent, making this option factually wrong. D is incorrect: Encryption is a separate Shield Platform Encryption feature unrelated to relationship type; master-detail provides no automatic encryption.,")
        Question(id: "19", question: "In designing account and opportunity team collaboration, which statements accurately describe how Account Teams and Opportunity Teams differ in what they affect? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Account Teams only affect list view visibility, while Opportunity Teams are the only mechanism that actually grants record-level sharing."), ("B", "Account Teams and Opportunity Teams are functionally identical and both automatically grant full access to every related object under the Account."), ("C", "Account Teams grant standing access to an Account and its related records for team members, while Opportunity Teams grant access scoped specifically to individual Opportunity records."), ("D", "A rep added only to an Opportunity Team won't automatically see the parent Account unless separately granted access through another mechanism."), ("E", "Opportunity Teams automatically add members to the Account Team, and vice versa, since they share the same underlying object.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Account Teams and Opportunity Teams are distinct sharing mechanisms scoped to their respective objects (C), and access granted through the Opportunity Team specifically does not automatically extend to the parent Account (D), which architects must design for explicitly. A is incorrect: Both mechanisms grant actual record-level sharing (not just list view visibility), contradicting this claim about Account Teams. B is incorrect: They are not identical; each grants access scoped to its own object and does not automatically cascade to unrelated records beyond configured settings. E is incorrect: They use related but separate underlying junction objects (AccountTeamMember, OpportunityTeamMember) and do not sync membership automatically.,")
        Question(id: "20", question: "What data model implication should an architect flag when a high-volume sales org wants to add every regional specialist to the Opportunity Team on thousands of Opportunities?", options: [("A", "Opportunity Teams are capped at exactly 3 members per Opportunity regardless of org edition, preventing this design outright."), ("B", "Opportunity Team membership has no data storage or performance impact since it is calculated on the fly at query time rather than stored."), ("C", "Each Opportunity Team membership record adds to the sharing table and DML overhead, so applying large team memberships across many Opportunities at scale can contribute to performance and sharing recalculation overhead similar to other data skew patterns."), ("D", "Adding Opportunity Team members does not affect sharing calculations at all, only Account Team members do.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "OpportunityTeamMember records are stored and drive sharing recalculation; applying broad team membership across a large volume of Opportunities increases the underlying sharing dataset and recalculation load, which is the same category of concern as other sharing/skew performance issues. A is incorrect: There is no fixed 3-member cap on Opportunity Teams; membership size is governed by license and practical performance limits, not a hard 3-person rule. B is incorrect: Team membership is persisted as actual records (not computed on the fly), so it does carry real storage and recalculation costs. D is incorrect: Opportunity Team memberships do drive their own sharing calculations independent of Account Teams; this claim incorrectly isolates the impact to only one mechanism.,")
        Question(id: "21", question: "An architect needs per-profile configuration values (e.g., discount tiers) that must be readable efficiently across many records without per-record queries, and separately needs a small reference list of shipping carriers usable by any user. Which combination is correct?", options: [("A", "Use a Hierarchy Custom Setting for the per-profile discount tiers (supports org/profile/user-level overrides) and a List Custom Setting for the shared shipping carrier reference data."), ("B", "Use a List Custom Setting for the per-profile discount tiers and a Hierarchy Custom Setting for the shipping carrier list, since carriers vary by region hierarchy."), ("C", "Use Custom Metadata Types for both use cases exclusively, since Custom Settings are deprecated and no longer supported on the platform."), ("D", "Use a single Hierarchy Custom Setting for both use cases, since Hierarchy Custom Settings support unlimited rows like List Custom Settings.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Hierarchy Custom Settings resolve to the most specific level (org, profile, user) making them ideal for tiered configuration like discount tiers, while List Custom Settings are better suited for a flat reference list like carriers, both cached and readable without SOQL. B is incorrect: List Custom Settings have no hierarchy resolution, so they cannot express profile/user-level discount overrides the way Hierarchy Custom Settings do. C is incorrect: Custom Settings are not deprecated; they remain a supported, distinct mechanism valued specifically for their cache-without-query access pattern. D is incorrect: Hierarchy Custom Settings are meant for tiered override values, not a flat list of many reference rows, which is what List Custom Settings are optimized for.,")
        Question(id: "22", question: "A company wants to display product inventory data that lives in an external SAP system inside Salesforce, without duplicating or persisting that data. What data modeling approach should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use a Big Object to store SAP inventory data since Big Objects are designed for external, non-persisted data."), ("B", "Create a custom object and use a nightly Bulk API job to sync all SAP inventory into Salesforce for local querying."), ("C", "Use Salesforce Connect with an external object mapped to the SAP data source via an OData or custom adapter, avoiding data duplication and storage consumption."), ("D", "Use a Heroku Connect mapping to replicate SAP data into a shared Postgres database that Salesforce queries live via callouts.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Salesforce Connect and external objects are purpose-built to virtualize external data — querying it live without persisting or consuming Salesforce storage, directly matching the stated requirement. A is incorrect: Big Objects are for large volumes of Salesforce-persisted data, not a virtualization layer over an external system. B is incorrect: A nightly Bulk API sync persists a full copy of SAP data in Salesforce storage, which is exactly what the requirement asks to avoid. D is incorrect: Heroku Connect syncs Salesforce data to Postgres (typically for Heroku app use), not the reverse virtualization of an external ERP into Salesforce records.,")
        Question(id: "23", question: "Sales Cloud and Service Cloud share the same org, and the architect must ensure a support agent viewing a Case can quickly see the customer's active Opportunities without building custom integration. What feature should be recommended?", options: [("A", "Add a related list for Opportunities on the Account page layout (or use Lightning App Builder to add an Opportunities component to the Case record page filtered by Account), leveraging the existing Account relationship shared by both clouds."), ("B", "Build a custom Apex REST integration that calls out to a separate Sales Cloud org to fetch Opportunity data in real time."), ("C", "Create a duplicate Opportunity custom object exclusively for Service Cloud users to avoid mixing Sales and Service data models."), ("D", "Use Data Loader to export Opportunities nightly into a Service Cloud-specific staging object.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Because both clouds share the same org and the same underlying Account object, native related lists and Lightning page components can surface Opportunity data directly on the Case page without any integration work. B is incorrect: A cross-org Apex REST integration is unnecessary overhead when Sales Cloud and Service Cloud share a single org and a common Account relationship already. C is incorrect: Duplicating Opportunity into a second object fragments the data model and loses native rollups, reports, and forecasting tied to the standard object. D is incorrect: A nightly staging sync introduces latency and redundant storage that a live related list avoids entirely.,")
        Question(id: "24", question: "A compliance team needs 7 years of change history on 45 fields for audit purposes, including detailed before/after values retained well beyond 18 months. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "A custom \"Audit Log\" object populated manually by users whenever they make a change to a tracked field."), ("B", "Standard Field History Tracking, since it can be configured to retain data indefinitely once the retention policy is set to \"Unlimited.\""), ("C", "Salesforce Shield Field Audit Trail, since it supports tracking more fields (up to 60\\) and retains history for up to 10 years, unlike standard Field History Tracking's 20-field/18-month limits."), ("D", "Salesforce Data Export scheduled weekly, storing historical CSV snapshots of the object as the audit trail.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Field Audit Trail is specifically designed to extend tracking capacity (60 fields) and retention (up to 10 years) well beyond standard Field History Tracking's 20-field, 18-month ceiling, directly matching the stated 45-field, 7-year requirement. A is incorrect: A manually populated custom object is unreliable and doesn't guarantee capture of every change, unlike platform-enforced automatic tracking. B is incorrect: Standard Field History Tracking has a hard 20-field limit and a fixed 18-month retention window in the UI; there is no \"Unlimited\" retention setting available on the standard feature. D is incorrect: Periodic CSV snapshots capture point-in-time states, not granular before/after field-level change events, and require manual reconciliation to reconstruct history.,")
        Question(id: "25", question: "An architect is deciding OWD for a new Invoice object expected to have millions of records, most of which need to be visible to all internal finance users, with occasional restriction needed for a handful of sensitive executive invoices. Which approaches help balance scalability with the exception case? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Set OWD to Private and create sharing rules to grant access to all finance users, since Private is always the safer default regardless of data volume."), ("B", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for all records, since finance users need visibility and any accidental edit can be reversed using field history."), ("C", "Layer a narrow, criteria-based sharing rule or restriction (e.g., a flag combined with a restriction mechanism) to explicitly handle the small sensitive subset rather than reversing the OWD for the whole object."), ("D", "Set OWD to Public Read Only for broad visibility, matching the common case where most Invoices should be visible to all finance users."), ("E", "Set OWD to Controlled by Parent, since Invoice records will always have a natural parent record providing appropriate default visibility.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Starting from a permissive OWD matching the dominant use case (D) and layering a narrow, targeted restriction only for the small sensitive subset (C) avoids the sharing recalculation overhead that a Private OWD covering nearly the entire object at massive volume would create. A is incorrect: Private OWD with sharing rules covering nearly the entire object at massive volume creates unnecessary sharing recalculation overhead for what is fundamentally a broad-access use case. B is incorrect: Public Read/Write removes any edit protection and does not address the requirement to restrict a sensitive subset at all. E is incorrect: Controlled by Parent requires an actual master-detail relationship to a parent object, and isn't guaranteed or automatically applicable without deliberately designing that relationship.,")
        Question(id: "26", question: "An Experience Cloud community with 2 million external customer users needs each customer to see only their own Cases and Orders. What OWD and design approach should the architect recommend to avoid sharing performance issues?", options: [("A", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for all external-facing objects, since Experience Cloud users are external and therefore lower risk than internal users."), ("B", "Set OWD to Private for Case and the external-facing custom objects, and rely on standard Experience Cloud sharing sets or sharing rules based on the customer's associated Account/Contact, avoiding broad manual sharing at that user scale."), ("C", "Set OWD to Private and manually share every Case and Order record to each individual external user via Apex-managed sharing at record creation."), ("D", "Disable sharing recalculation entirely for external users since Experience Cloud licenses are not counted toward standard sharing limits.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Private OWD combined with sharing sets (which map an external user's profile/permission set to their own related records via a matching criteria) is the platform-native way to isolate millions of external users' data without generating a massive volume of individual manual shares. A is incorrect: Public Read/Write would expose every customer's Cases and Orders to every other customer, violating the stated data isolation requirement entirely. C is incorrect: Explicit Apex-managed sharing for millions of individual external users at scale creates enormous sharing table volume and recalculation cost compared to using sharing sets. D is incorrect: There is no such thing as disabling sharing recalculation for external users only; sharing rules and recalculation apply uniformly regardless of license type.,")
        Question(id: "27", question: "A logistics company wants a business data dictionary that stays synchronized with the org's actual field metadata as the schema evolves across quarterly releases. What approach should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use tools that read Salesforce metadata (e.g., via the Metadata API or Setup's Schema Builder/Object Manager export) to auto-generate and refresh the dictionary, rather than maintaining a fully separate manually written document."), ("B", "Maintain the data dictionary exclusively in a static spreadsheet updated once a year during the annual architecture review."), ("C", "Rely solely on Salesforce's built-in field-level help text as the complete data dictionary, since it is visible to end users."), ("D", "Avoid maintaining a data dictionary altogether since Salesforce's Setup Object Manager already serves as the dictionary for both business and technical audiences equally well.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Deriving the dictionary from actual metadata (via API or schema export tooling) keeps business documentation aligned with technical reality as fields and objects change each release, rather than letting it drift out of sync. B is incorrect: An annual manual refresh will drift significantly out of sync with quarterly schema changes, defeating the purpose of a living data dictionary. C is incorrect: Help text is a useful but incomplete and often inconsistently populated source; it cannot substitute for a structured, comprehensive data dictionary. D is incorrect: Object Manager shows technical metadata but lacks business-friendly context (definitions, ownership, classification) that a proper data dictionary requires for non-technical stakeholders.,")
        Question(id: "28", question: "A company wants to tag fields containing personally identifiable information (PII) so downstream governance tooling can identify and apply masking policies. What native Salesforce feature should the architect recommend as the foundation?", options: [("A", "Custom picklist fields added to every object to manually track PII status, maintained entirely outside of Setup metadata."), ("B", "Field-level Data Classification metadata (Classification, Compliance Categorization) available in Setup, which can be applied per field and queried/reported on for governance purposes."), ("C", "Field-level security profiles alone, since restricting visibility is functionally equivalent to classifying data as sensitive."), ("D", "Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption applied universally, since encrypting a field is itself considered classification.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Salesforce provides native Data Classification metadata fields (Classification and Compliance Categorization) specifically designed to tag data sensitivity at the field level, which governance tooling and reporting can then act on. A is incorrect: Building parallel custom tracking fields duplicates functionality Salesforce already provides natively as field metadata, adding unnecessary maintenance. C is incorrect: Field-level security controls who can see a field; it does not itself record or communicate the field's data sensitivity classification for governance tooling. D is incorrect: Encryption protects data at rest but does not serve as a classification label; a field can be sensitive without being encrypted, and vice versa isn't guaranteed either.,")
        Question(id: "29", question: "What downstream governance benefit does field-level Data Classification provide once PII fields are tagged, beyond simple documentation?", options: [("A", "Data Classification metadata is only visible in Setup and cannot be queried, reported on, or used by any automation or governance tooling."), ("B", "Data Classification automatically enforces field-level security so classified fields become inaccessible to any user without System Administrator profile."), ("C", "Data Classification automatically triggers Shield Platform Encryption on any field marked as \"Sensitive,\" removing the need for separate encryption configuration."), ("D", "Classification metadata can be queried and reported on to drive automated compliance reviews, feed data masking/sandbox seeding decisions, and support impact analysis before schema changes affecting sensitive fields.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Because classification metadata is queryable (via Metadata API/Tooling API) and reportable, governance programs can build automated processes around it — such as flagging sensitive fields before sandbox refreshes or schema changes — rather than relying on tribal knowledge. A is incorrect: Classification metadata is accessible via the Tooling API and can be incorporated into custom reporting and governance automation, contradicting this claim. B is incorrect: Classification does not automatically change field-level security; access control remains a separate, manually configured layer. C is incorrect: Classification is metadata only; it does not automatically activate encryption, which must be separately configured through Shield.,")
        Question(id: "30", question: "A retailer needs to retain 3 billion point-of-sale transaction records for 10 years for audit purposes, rarely queried except by exact transaction ID and date range, and must avoid consuming standard data storage allocations. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "A Big Object with a composite index built with transaction ID and date as the leading index fields, since Big Objects support massive volume outside standard data storage limits and are queried efficiently when the index's leading field is used for exact-match lookups."), ("B", "A standard custom object with Field History Tracking enabled, since standard objects support unlimited storage once Shield is purchased."), ("C", "A Big Object queried primarily through report filters and list views, since these interfaces provide the most flexible ad hoc query capability for Big Objects."), ("D", "An External Object via Salesforce Connect pointed at an internal staging table, since External Objects never count against any storage limit and support full SOQL and SOSL.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Big Objects are designed exactly for this profile — massive volume, long retention, storage outside standard limits — and their index-based query model performs well when queries lead with the indexed field(s), such as transaction ID and date here. B is incorrect: Standard objects still consume data storage regardless of Shield licensing; there is no unlimited storage tier unlocked by Shield alone. C is incorrect: Big Objects have limited support in standard reports and list views; they are best queried via SOQL or Apex against the defined index, not general ad hoc report filtering. D is incorrect: External Objects have real query limitations (no SOSL, restricted SOQL) and depend on the availability/performance of the external system; they are not exempt from all constraints as claimed.,")
        Question(id: "31", question: "A global manufacturer sources customer data from CRM, ERP, and e-commerce, and wants Salesforce to remain authoritative for financial account data while other systems keep authority over their own domains. Which MDM implementation style fits best?", options: [("A", "Registry, since a lightweight index requires no ongoing synchronization between systems and works when no system is authoritative for anything."), ("B", "Coexistence, since each system retains authority over its own domain while Salesforce and other systems exchange updates to keep a consistent shared view without one central hub owning everything."), ("C", "Centralized Hub, since all customer data should always be consolidated into Salesforce regardless of where a given attribute is authoritative."), ("D", "Consolidation, since a nightly batch copy into Salesforce automatically resolves all authority conflicts between source systems.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Coexistence explicitly supports multiple systems each remaining authoritative for their own domain of data (e.g., ERP for financials) while still exchanging and synchronizing shared attributes, matching the stated requirement precisely. A is incorrect: Registry style only stores pointers/keys to source records without owning or synchronizing data, so it doesn't support Salesforce having authority for a domain as stated. C is incorrect: Centralized Hub replaces this distributed authority model entirely, requiring Salesforce (or an MDM hub) to be the sole owner across all domains, which contradicts the given constraint. D is incorrect: Consolidation copies data into a golden record store but doesn't inherently preserve domain-specific external authority without additional survivorship configuration; this option overstates automatic conflict resolution.,")
        Question(id: "32", question: "Three source systems supply conflicting mailing addresses for the same customer, and the business wants the freshest, most complete address to win automatically. What survivorship approach should the architect design?", options: [("A", "Randomly select one of the three values on each save to distribute data quality risk evenly across systems."), ("B", "Always trust whichever system alphabetically comes first by system name, since this avoids the need for any comparison logic."), ("C", "A rules-based survivorship strategy combining a recency check (most recently updated timestamp) with a completeness check (fewest blank sub-fields), applied per attribute rather than picking one system as universally authoritative."), ("D", "Always trust the system with the largest total record count in the org, since more records implies more reliable data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Combining most-recent-wins and most-complete-wins criteria at the attribute level is a standard, defensible survivorship pattern that directly reflects the stated business goal of freshest and most complete data. A is incorrect: Random selection introduces unpredictable inconsistency and defeats the purpose of a deliberate survivorship strategy. B is incorrect: Alphabetical system name has no relationship to data freshness or completeness and would produce arbitrary, unreliable results. D is incorrect: Total record count in a system says nothing about the quality or recency of any individual customer's address field.,")
        Question(id: "33", question: "When two duplicate Account records are merged using Salesforce's standard Merge Accounts feature, which outcomes accurately describe what happens to child records and sharing grants of the losing (non-surviving) record? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Child records are deleted along with the losing Account, requiring a full data reload from backup if they are needed again."), ("B", "Child records (e.g., Contacts, Opportunities, Cases) are automatically reparented to the surviving Account as part of the merge transaction."), ("C", "Manually created sharing grants specific to the losing record are not guaranteed to be preserved and may need to be recreated if still needed."), ("D", "All sharing grants, both automatic and manually created, transfer perfectly intact to the surviving record with no gaps."), ("E", "Child records remain attached to the deleted Account's ID indefinitely in a hidden state, invisible to all users until manually reparented via Data Loader.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Standard merge reparents related child records to the surviving Account automatically (B), but explicit manual sharing grants tied to the merged-away record are a recognized gap that isn't guaranteed to carry over (C), which architects must account for in a golden-record strategy. A is incorrect: Child records are not deleted during a merge; they are reparented to the surviving record, preserving relational integrity. D is incorrect: Manual sharing grants specific to the losing record are a recognized gap in merge behavior, not something guaranteed to transfer perfectly. E is incorrect: There is no hidden orphaned state; reparenting happens immediately as part of the merge transaction.,")
        Question(id: "34", question: "A company wants source system cross-references maintained on each Salesforce Account so downstream systems can trace which CRM, ERP, or e-commerce record contributed to the golden record. What data model approach should be recommended?", options: [("A", "Avoid storing any external keys and instead match on Account Name at integration time to reduce field count."), ("B", "Overwrite the Salesforce Account ID with the ERP system's customer number so all systems share one consistent identifier."), ("C", "Store only the most recently integrated source system's key, discarding the others since a golden record should have exactly one external reference."), ("D", "Store per-source External ID fields (or a related cross-reference child object) on the Account that capture each source system's native key, preserving bidirectional traceability without overwriting the golden Account's own Salesforce ID.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Preserving each source system's native key (via distinct External ID fields or a cross-reference child object) is exactly how bidirectional traceability and re-processing are supported without disturbing the Salesforce record's own identity. A is incorrect: Matching on Account Name at integration time is unreliable (names change, are duplicated, or vary in formatting) and is not a substitute for a stable external key. B is incorrect: Salesforce's own record ID cannot be overwritten with an external value, and doing so would break internal relationship integrity across the org. C is incorrect: Discarding all but the most recent source key eliminates traceability to the other contributing systems, defeating the stated requirement.,")
        Question(id: "35", question: "A customer wants a real-time, low-latency master data update process where Salesforce simply indexes pointers to records that remain physically stored and updated in each source system. Which MDM style and trade-off applies?", options: [("A", "Centralized Hub style, which always requires Salesforce to become the sole system of record for every attribute, eliminating the need for any pointer-based architecture."), ("B", "Consolidation style, which stores a full physical golden copy in Salesforce and therefore requires no connectivity back to source systems ever."), ("C", "Registry style, which avoids data duplication and keeps updates fast at the source, but requires real-time or near-real-time connectivity back to each source system whenever the full record detail is needed in Salesforce."), ("D", "Coexistence style, which mandates that every source system be retired within 90 days of implementation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Registry style is defined by storing lightweight index/pointer records rather than full copies, which minimizes duplication but creates a dependency on live connectivity to source systems for full detail retrieval, matching the described trade-off exactly. A is incorrect: Centralized Hub does not preclude pointer architectures categorically, and requiring Salesforce to own every attribute is not implied by the scenario's registry-style description. B is incorrect: Consolidation does store a full physical copy, but that's the opposite of the pointer-based, source-remains-authoritative model described in the scenario. D is incorrect: Coexistence has no such 90-day retirement requirement; it's characterized by systems continuing to coexist, not being retired on a fixed timeline.,")
        Question(id: "36", question: "Which combination of native Salesforce tools most directly supports maintaining a golden record within a single org over time as new records are created by multiple integration channels? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Increasing the org's data storage allocation to accommodate duplicate records without cleanup"), ("B", "Duplicate Rules configured to Block or Alert on likely duplicates at the point of creation"), ("C", "Matching Rules that define the fuzzy/exact logic used to detect potential duplicate records"), ("D", "Chatter feed tracking on the Account object to notify users of new related Cases"), ("E", "Person Accounts, since enabling them automatically prevents all duplicate creation org-wide")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Matching Rules define the detection logic (exact/fuzzy matching criteria) and Duplicate Rules act on that logic at save time (blocking or alerting), together forming the native mechanism for preventing new duplicates from undermining the golden record as multiple channels create data. A is incorrect: Increasing storage allocation accommodates more duplicates rather than preventing or resolving them, working against the golden record goal. D is incorrect: Chatter feed tracking notifies users of activity but has no role in duplicate detection or prevention logic. E is incorrect: Person Accounts change the Account/Contact data model but provide no automatic duplicate prevention capability by themselves.,")
        Question(id: "37", question: "A company has 200 Service Cloud agents needing full case and knowledge access, and 1,000 internal employees who only need to view and edit records on two custom objects with no need for cases, opportunities, or standard CRM objects. What licensing and data model approach minimizes cost while meeting access needs?", options: [("A", "Assign Chatter Free licenses to the 1,000 internal employees, since Chatter Free licenses include full read/write access to custom objects."), ("B", "Assign full Salesforce licenses to all 1,200 users, since Platform licenses cannot access any custom objects at all."), ("C", "Assign Service Cloud licenses to all 1,200 users, since Service Cloud licenses are the cheapest license type available for any internal user."), ("D", "Assign Service Cloud licenses to the 200 agents and Salesforce Platform licenses to the 1,000 internal employees, since Platform licenses support custom object access without the cost of full CRM feature access those users don't need.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Salesforce Platform licenses are specifically designed for users who need custom object and app access without full CRM (Sales/Service Cloud) functionality, making them the cost-appropriate choice for the 1,000 employees while Service Cloud covers the agents who need case/knowledge features. A is incorrect: Chatter Free licenses provide only Chatter collaboration features and do not grant read/write access to standard or custom object records. B is incorrect: Platform licenses do support custom object access; this claim about a lack of custom object access is factually incorrect and leads to needless overspending on full licenses. C is incorrect: Service Cloud licenses cost more than Platform licenses and include case/knowledge features unnecessary for users who only need two custom objects.,")
        Question(id: "38", question: "A company wants to give 500 external partners read-only access to a subset of Account and Case fields, minimizing license cost. Which license type and design approach best fits?", options: [("A", "Partner Community (now Experience Cloud Partner) licenses combined with sharing sets or sharing rules scoped to only the necessary fields and records, avoiding full internal Salesforce licenses for external users."), ("B", "Full Salesforce licenses for all 500 partners, since only full licenses can access Account and Case objects at all."), ("C", "Chatter External licenses, since these licenses grant full record-level access to any standard object shared with the external user."), ("D", "Guest User licenses on a public site, since Guest access is designed for authenticated, individually identifiable partner data access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Experience Cloud partner-oriented licenses are purpose-built and priced for external users needing scoped access to specific objects like Account and Case, making them far more cost-effective than full internal licenses for this read-only partner use case. B is incorrect: Full Salesforce licenses are unnecessarily expensive for this use case; Experience Cloud licenses support Account/Case access at a lower cost point for external users. C is incorrect: Chatter External licenses are limited to Chatter collaboration and do not grant general record-level access to standard objects like Account and Case. D is incorrect: Guest User access is for unauthenticated public site visitors, not appropriate for identifiable, persistent partner accounts needing scoped record access.,")
        Question(id: "39", question: "An architect needs to enforce that every Contact record has a valid email format and a populated Lead Source field, regardless of whether the record is created via the UI, API, or Data Loader. What combination of tools ensures this consistently across all channels?", options: [("A", "A screen flow with input validation shown only when users create Contacts through the standard New button."), ("B", "A required field on the page layout only, since page layout-level requiredness is enforced identically across the API and Data Loader."), ("C", "A validation rule checking email format and Lead Source population, since validation rules execute for all save contexts (UI, API, Bulk API respecting settings) unlike a Lightning Component-based check that only fires in the UI."), ("D", "Client-side JavaScript validation embedded in a custom Visualforce page used only by the sales team.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Validation rules execute at the database layer for every save, regardless of the interface used (UI, REST/SOAP API, Bulk API, Data Loader), making them the only listed option that reliably enforces the rule across every channel. A is incorrect: A screen flow only fires when a user explicitly walks through that specific UI path, leaving API and Data Loader inserts completely unvalidated. B is incorrect: Page layout-level \"required\" only affects the UI rendering of that layout; it does not block API or Data Loader inserts that bypass the layout entirely. D is incorrect: Client-side JavaScript in a Visualforce page only affects that specific page's UI and has zero effect on API-based or Data Loader-based record creation.,")
        Question(id: "40", question: "When loading 50,000 Contact records via Data Loader with duplicate rules configured to Block on the Contact object, what risk should the architect flag for the load process?", options: [("A", "Duplicate Rules have zero performance impact on Data Loader batch jobs since they only evaluate synchronously in the UI, never during API-based inserts."), ("B", "Duplicate Rule evaluation for every record adds processing overhead and can significantly slow or partially fail large batch loads, so the architect should consider using Bulk API with duplicate rules temporarily adjusted to Alert or bypassed for the initial load, followed by a separate dedupe pass."), ("C", "Duplicate Rules automatically pause during any Data Loader session by default, requiring no special configuration."), ("D", "Duplicate Rules set to Block will silently skip duplicate records without generating any error rows in the Data Loader results file.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Duplicate Rules evaluate on every insert/update regardless of channel, including Data Loader, and at high volume this can meaningfully slow the load or cause unexpected partial failures, so architects commonly recommend adjusting the rule action or duplicate checking strategy for large one-time loads. A is incorrect: Duplicate Rules do evaluate during API-based inserts including Data Loader, contradicting this claim of UI-only evaluation. C is incorrect: There is no automatic pause of Duplicate Rules during Data Loader sessions; any adjustment must be explicitly configured beforehand. D is incorrect: A Block action generates explicit error rows identifying the duplicate conflict; it does not silently skip records without any feedback.,")
        Question(id: "41", question: "A company operates two Salesforce orgs (EMEA and North America Sales Cloud) and needs a real-time unified customer view without merging the orgs. Which characteristics accurately describe a real-time federation approach (point-to-point APIs or Salesforce Connect Cross-Org Adapter) for this need? (Choose 3\\)", options: [("A", "It guarantees identical query performance to a single-org data model with zero added latency under any circumstances."), ("B", "It requires no configuration of which objects or fields are exposed across orgs, working automatically for any data."), ("C", "It is a materially faster path to a unified view than a multi-year full org consolidation project."), ("D", "It avoids the cost and disruption of consolidating two live orgs into one."), ("E", "It introduces some query latency and a dependency on both orgs' availability at query time compared to a single local dataset.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3, 4], explanation: "Federating live access (D) trades some latency and uptime dependency on both orgs (E) for a much faster time-to-value than full consolidation (C), which is the honest, standard trade-off architects present for this scenario. A is incorrect: Federated cross-org queries add real network and processing latency compared to a single local dataset; performance is not guaranteed to be identical. B is incorrect: Both point-to-point APIs and the Cross-Org Adapter require explicit configuration of which objects/fields are exposed and mapped; it is not automatic for arbitrary data.,")
        Question(id: "42", question: "What is the primary limitation of Salesforce-to-Salesforce (S2S) compared to a custom API-based replication solution when sharing Opportunity data between two partner orgs?", options: [("A", "S2S can only share Leads and Cases, never Opportunities, making it entirely unusable for this scenario."), ("B", "S2S only supports publishing/subscribing to a defined set of standard and custom objects with field-level mapping configured per connection, and lacks the full bidirectional, arbitrarily complex transformation logic a custom API integration can implement."), ("C", "S2S requires both orgs to be on the same Salesforce instance (pod), making it unusable for geographically separated orgs."), ("D", "S2S has no field-level mapping capability at all and can only share entire records with every field exposed identically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "S2S does support Opportunity sharing but is constrained to configured object/field publish-subscribe mappings without custom transformation logic, which is exactly the limitation that pushes complex integration needs toward custom API-based replication instead. A is incorrect: S2S does support Opportunities among its shareable standard objects; this blanket restriction to Leads and Cases only is incorrect. C is incorrect: S2S works across different orgs regardless of pod/instance location; there's no same-pod requirement. D is incorrect: S2S does support field-level mapping configuration per connection, allowing selective field sharing rather than an all-or-nothing record share.,")
        Question(id: "43", question: "Two Salesforce orgs are being consolidated into one master org, and both contain overlapping Account and Contact records for the same real-world customers. What strategy best prevents duplicate creation while preserving referential integrity for related Opportunities and Cases?", options: [("A", "Skip Account and Contact migration entirely and only migrate Opportunities and Cases, since parent records can be recreated automatically by Salesforce during related-record insert."), ("B", "Load both orgs' data independently with auto-generated new Salesforce IDs and rely on users to manually identify and merge duplicates after go-live."), ("C", "Establish a cross-org External ID mapping (source org record ID stored as an External ID field) before migration, use it to detect and merge overlapping Accounts/Contacts first, then remap child Opportunity/Case foreign keys to the surviving records during load."), ("D", "Rename all Account records from one org with a prefix to guarantee no name-based duplicate detection ever triggers, avoiding the need for any reconciliation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Capturing each source org's record ID as an External ID field enables reliable, traceable identification and merging of overlapping parent records before migration, and that same mapping is used to correctly reparent child Opportunity/Case records to the surviving Account/Contact, preserving referential integrity. A is incorrect: Salesforce does not auto-create missing parent Account/Contact records during a child object insert; parent records must exist and be referenced explicitly. B is incorrect: Manual post-go-live merging at scale is error-prone and leaves the org in an inconsistent, duplicate-heavy state for an extended period, which the architecture should proactively prevent. D is incorrect: Deliberately avoiding duplicate detection by renaming records defeats the goal of preventing duplicates and guarantees a fragmented customer view post-merge.,")
        Question(id: "44", question: "A single view of the customer must combine data from Sales Cloud, Service Cloud, and a separate Marketing Cloud instance. What is the key architectural trade-off between materializing this view as persisted Salesforce records versus computing it on-demand via live API calls?", options: [("A", "Materializing the view improves query performance and offline/report availability at the cost of sync latency and additional storage, while on-demand computation avoids staleness and storage cost but depends on the availability and response time of every source system at query time."), ("B", "There is no meaningful trade-off, since both approaches deliver identical real-time accuracy and performance in all cases."), ("C", "On-demand computation always uses less API call volume than materialization, since materialization requires continuous polling of every source system every few seconds."), ("D", "Materialized views cannot be used in standard Salesforce reports, making on-demand computation the only reportable option.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This is the standard architectural trade-off: persisted/materialized data supports fast queries and reporting but can go stale and consumes storage, while live on-demand computation is always current but introduces a hard dependency on every source system's uptime and latency at query time. B is incorrect: The two approaches have materially different performance, freshness, and dependency characteristics; claiming they're identical ignores the core trade-off being tested. C is incorrect: Materialization is typically driven by event-based sync (e.g., Platform Events, Change Data Capture) rather than constant polling, and can actually use less overall API volume than repeated live queries per view. D is incorrect: Materialized (persisted) Salesforce records are fully reportable using standard reports; it's actually the on-demand/virtualized data that has more reporting limitations.,")
        Question(id: "45", question: "How does Salesforce Data Cloud differ architecturally from a custom unified profile built on standard Salesforce objects for creating a single view of the customer across many interaction systems?", options: [("A", "Data Cloud ingests and harmonizes high-volume, high-velocity data from many sources into its own scalable data model with identity resolution, without necessarily persisting everything as standard Salesforce records, whereas a custom unified profile is built and maintained entirely within standard object storage and automation."), ("B", "Data Cloud and a custom unified profile are functionally identical, differing only in licensing cost, with no architectural distinction in how data is ingested or resolved."), ("C", "Data Cloud requires all source data to first be loaded into standard Salesforce custom objects before it can be used, making it strictly a reporting layer on top of existing objects."), ("D", "A custom unified profile scales better than Data Cloud for high-volume, high-velocity multi-source identity resolution because it uses native Salesforce storage.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Data Cloud is purpose-built for large-scale, multi-source data harmonization and identity resolution outside the constraints of standard Salesforce object storage and governor limits, which is a fundamentally different architecture than manually building and maintaining a unified profile using custom objects, triggers, and flows. B is incorrect: The two approaches differ significantly in ingestion architecture, scalability, and identity resolution capability, not just licensing; this claim understates a core architectural distinction. C is incorrect: Data Cloud connects directly to many source systems (including non-Salesforce sources) without requiring pre-loading into standard Salesforce custom objects first. D is incorrect: Custom unified profiles built on standard objects are constrained by standard platform storage and governor limits, making them generally less scalable for high-volume, high-velocity identity resolution than Data Cloud's purpose-built architecture.,")
        Question(id: "46", question: "What are the risks of using the Bulk API with allOrNone=false while simultaneously loading Accounts and their child Contacts in the same integration run?", options: [("A", "If some Account records fail while their corresponding Contact records still attempt to insert referencing the failed Account's expected ID, those Contact records will fail too, but with allOrNone=false the successful, unrelated records still commit, potentially leaving a partially loaded and referentially inconsistent dataset that requires careful reconciliation."), ("B", "allOrNone=false guarantees that no orphaned Contact records can ever be created, since Salesforce automatically rolls back any batch containing a referential failure."), ("C", "allOrNone=false only applies to Contacts, not Accounts, so all Account records always succeed regardless of failures elsewhere in the batch."), ("D", "Using allOrNone=false has no impact on referential integrity since Bulk API always processes parent and child objects in a single atomic transaction.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "With allOrNone=false, each record in a batch succeeds or fails independently; if a parent Account load fails, dependent Contact inserts referencing that Account will also fail, but other unrelated successful records still commit, producing a partial, potentially inconsistent dataset that needs reconciliation. B is incorrect: There's no automatic full-batch rollback with allOrNone=false; that behavior describes allOrNone=true, which enforces atomicity at the batch level instead. C is incorrect: allOrNone applies uniformly to whichever object's batch it's configured on, not selectively to just child objects. D is incorrect: Bulk API does not treat separate parent and child load jobs as one atomic transaction; each Bulk API job/batch is processed independently, which is exactly why referential risk exists across separate Account and Contact loads.,")
        Question(id: "47", question: "How does Change Data Capture (CDC) support downstream data consistency for an external system needing near-real-time notification of Opportunity changes, and what are its limitations compared to synchronous after-insert/after-update triggers? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "CDC replaces the need for any object to have field history tracking, since CDC captures the exact same historical range indefinitely."), ("B", "CDC events can be consumed only by other Salesforce orgs, never by external, non-Salesforce systems."), ("C", "Because CDC is asynchronous, it cannot block or influence the original transaction the way a synchronous Apex trigger can."), ("D", "CDC publishes change events asynchronously to a durable event bus that external subscribers can reliably consume even if briefly offline."), ("E", "CDC guarantees synchronous delivery to all subscribers before the originating transaction commits, functioning identically to a before-save trigger.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "CDC's asynchronous, durable event-based delivery (D) is well suited for near-real-time external notification without transactional dependency on the save, but that same asynchronicity means it cannot enforce validation or block the original transaction (C) the way a synchronous trigger can. A is incorrect: CDC event retention is time-limited (a rolling window, not indefinite), so it does not substitute for the longer-term retention that Field History Tracking or Field Audit Trail provide. B is incorrect: CDC events can be consumed by external systems via API (e.g., using the Pub/Sub API), not just other Salesforce orgs. E is incorrect: CDC delivery is asynchronous and decoupled from the original transaction; it does not block or execute synchronously like a before-save trigger.,")
        Question(id: "48", question: "A company wants Duplicate Management configured so that obvious exact-match duplicates are blocked at save time, but likely fuzzy matches are flagged for manual review rather than blocked outright. What configuration should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Disable Duplicate Rules for fuzzy matches entirely, since Duplicate Management does not support any confidence-based distinction between match types."), ("B", "Use a single Duplicate Rule with a single Matching Rule set to Block, since one rule can express both blocking and alerting behavior simultaneously for different match confidence levels."), ("C", "Configure the Matching Rule to Block and the Duplicate Rule to Alert, since Matching Rules control the save-time action and Duplicate Rules only control detection logic."), ("D", "Create two Matching Rules (one strict exact-match, one fuzzy) tied to two separate Duplicate Rules, setting the exact-match rule's action to Block and the fuzzy rule's action to Alert, so each match type triggers the appropriate response independently.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Because Duplicate Rules can each carry their own action (Block or Alert) and reference distinct Matching Rules, using two separate rule pairs — one strict/Block, one fuzzy/Alert — is the native way to apply different responses based on match confidence. A is incorrect: Duplicate Management does support multiple rules with independent actions, so confidence-based differentiation is achievable natively, contrary to this claim. B is incorrect: A single Duplicate Rule has one action setting; it cannot apply Block to some matches and Alert to others within the same rule. C is incorrect: This reverses the actual roles — Matching Rules define detection logic (the \"how\"), while Duplicate Rules define the save-time action (Block/Alert), not the other way around.,")
        Question(id: "49", question: "A nonprofit's Salesforce org integrates with a grants management system, a volunteer portal, and an email marketing tool, each writing to the same Contact object via separate scheduled jobs. Records occasionally get corrupted mid-process when two jobs update the same Contact concurrently. What pattern addresses this?", options: [("A", "Switch all three integrations to use Bulk API exclusively, since Bulk API guarantees no two jobs can ever update the same record concurrently."), ("B", "Add more validation rules to the Contact object, since validation rules automatically serialize concurrent DML operations from external systems."), ("C", "Increase the org's API request limit, since higher limits eliminate any possibility of concurrent write conflicts."), ("D", "Use Apex-based upsert logic with row-level locking (e.g., FOR UPDATE in SOQL) or a coordinated integration queue/middleware layer to serialize concurrent writes to the same Contact, preventing race conditions between the three jobs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Explicit row-locking in Apex or a middleware coordination layer directly addresses the root cause — concurrent, uncoordinated writes to the same record — by serializing access, which is the standard pattern for resolving multi-system write contention. A is incorrect: Bulk API processes batches asynchronously and does not inherently prevent two separate Bulk API jobs (or a Bulk job and a real-time API call) from touching the same record concurrently. B is incorrect: Validation rules check data correctness at save time; they have no mechanism to serialize or coordinate concurrent transactions from different systems. C is incorrect: API request limits govern call volume, not concurrency control; raising them does nothing to prevent simultaneous writes to the same record.,")
        Question(id: "50", question: "An architect is asked to recommend an approach ensuring that data entered via a Flow-based screen matches the same consistency rules enforced by a REST API integration for the same object. What should be emphasized?", options: [("A", "Centralizing consistency logic in validation rules and/or a common Apex service class invoked by both the Flow and the API-facing Apex REST class, rather than duplicating logic separately in the Flow's own validation elements and the integration code."), ("B", "Building the consistency checks twice — once in Flow validation elements and once in the Apex REST class — since each interface requires entirely separate, non-reusable logic."), ("C", "Relying only on page layout \"required\" field settings, since these apply uniformly to both Flow screens and REST API calls."), ("D", "Skipping validation for the API integration entirely, since external systems are assumed to send only clean data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Centralizing the actual consistency rules at the database layer (validation rules) or in a shared Apex service invoked by both entry points guarantees identical enforcement regardless of which interface is used, avoiding logic drift between duplicated implementations. B is incorrect: Duplicating logic across Flow and Apex REST code creates a maintenance risk where the two implementations can drift out of sync over time, undermining consistency. C is incorrect: Page layout \"required\" settings are UI-only and have no effect on records created via the Apex REST API, which bypasses layouts entirely. D is incorrect: Assuming external data is always clean is precisely the risk validation exists to mitigate; skipping it defeats the stated consistency goal.,")
        Question(id: "51", question: "A services company wants case status changes and case comments visible to both internal agents and external Experience Cloud customers, but the two audiences should never see each other's internal-only notes on the same case timeline. What data model feature enables this distinction?", options: [("A", "Use the standard Case Comments \"Public\" checkbox (or equivalent internal/external flag) to control which comments are visible to the customer community versus internal-only, rather than creating fully separate comment objects for each audience."), ("B", "Create two completely separate Case objects, one for internal support and one for customer-facing tracking, and manually sync status between them."), ("C", "Use Field-Level Security to hide the entire Case object from the External Community Profile, thereby preventing this requirement from being met at all."), ("D", "Rely on Chatter free-text posts only, since Case Comments have no concept of internal versus external visibility.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The standard Case Comment object's public/internal flag is purpose-built to let a single case record and comment thread serve both audiences while controlling exactly which comments are customer-visible, avoiding duplicated case data. B is incorrect: Maintaining two synchronized Case objects for the same underlying case introduces significant complexity and sync risk compared to using the native public/internal comment flag on one object. C is incorrect: Hiding the Case object entirely from the external profile would prevent customers from seeing their case at all, which contradicts the stated requirement that both audiences see case status. D is incorrect: Case Comments do have a native public/internal distinction; this claim about the feature not existing is factually incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "52", question: "What is a key consideration when an org's automation-heavy data consistency strategy relies heavily on Apex triggers across multiple objects, and a bulk data load of 200,000 records begins hitting mixed DML and CPU-time governor limit errors?", options: [("A", "Switching from Bulk API to Data Loader with a batch size of 1 will bypass governor limits entirely for trigger-heavy objects."), ("B", "Governor limits do not apply to Apex triggers fired during Bulk API loads, so the errors must originate from an unrelated source."), ("C", "The correct fix is always to disable all triggers permanently for large loads and accept any resulting data inconsistency as a normal trade-off."), ("D", "Trigger logic should be reviewed for bulkification (avoiding per-record SOQL/DML inside loops) and, where appropriate, some validation can be moved to database-layer validation rules which are generally more efficient at very high volumes than equivalent Apex trigger logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Non-bulkified trigger logic (queries or DML inside loops) is a classic cause of governor limit failures at high volume, and shifting simple consistency checks to validation rules — which run more efficiently at scale — alongside proper trigger bulkification is the standard architectural remediation. A is incorrect: Governor limits (per-transaction limits) apply regardless of batch size; reducing batch size to 1 does not exempt a transaction from CPU time or DML limits and would drastically slow the load. B is incorrect: Governor limits fully apply to Apex triggers regardless of whether the DML originates from Bulk API, Data Loader, or the UI; this claim is factually incorrect. C is incorrect: Permanently disabling triggers sacrifices necessary business logic and data consistency rather than addressing the actual bulkification problem, and isn't the appropriate general fix.,")
        Question(id: "53", question: "A logistics company's data model includes a custom Shipment object referencing Account via lookup. The team wants Shipment records to inherit the same OWD-based default visibility as their parent Account without maintaining separate sharing rules. Which statements are accurate considerations for this design? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Keeping the lookup relationship achieves identical automatic visibility inheritance to master-detail, since \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" applies equally to both relationship types."), ("B", "Changing the Shipment-to-Account relationship to master-detail (if feasible given ownership/business needs) is the only relationship type providing automatic \"Controlled by Parent\" visibility inheritance without separate sharing rules."), ("C", "A formula field referencing the Account owner is sufficient to replicate access-level inheritance."), ("D", "Setting Shipment's OWD to Public Read/Write achieves the same practical effect as inheriting Account's visibility without any relationship changes."), ("E", "If master-detail isn't feasible due to ownership requirements, the team should plan for explicit sharing rules or Apex-managed sharing to approximate the same effect on the lookup relationship.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "\"Controlled by Parent\" sharing is only available through master-detail (B); when that conversion isn't feasible, the realistic fallback is explicit sharing rules or Apex-managed sharing to approximate the same inherited visibility on a lookup relationship (E). A is incorrect: \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" and implicit parent-based sharing are properties of master-detail relationships, not standard lookups, so this claim misapplies that inheritance behavior. C is incorrect: A formula field can display owner information but has no mechanism to grant or change record-level access; it cannot replicate sharing inheritance. D is incorrect: Public Read/Write grants universal access regardless of Account ownership, which is a fundamentally different (and less controlled) visibility model than true parent-based inheritance.,")
        Question(id: "54", question: "A logistics company's data model includes a custom Shipment object referencing Account via lookup. The team wants Shipment records to inherit the same OWD-based default visibility as their parent Account without maintaining separate sharing rules. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use a formula field referencing the Account owner, since formula fields alone are sufficient to replicate access-level inheritance."), ("B", "Keep the lookup relationship and rely on Apex-shared \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" automatically applying to lookup relationships identically to master-detail."), ("C", "Set Shipment's OWD to Public Read/Write, since this achieves the same practical effect as inheriting Account's visibility without any relationship changes."), ("D", "Change the Shipment-to-Account relationship to master-detail (if feasible given ownership/business needs), since master-detail is the only relationship type providing automatic \"Controlled by Parent\" visibility inheritance without separate sharing rules.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "\"Controlled by Parent\" sharing behavior, which mirrors the child's visibility to the parent Account automatically, is only available through a master-detail relationship; a lookup relationship requires explicit sharing rules or Apex sharing to approximate the same effect. A is incorrect: A formula field can display owner information but has no mechanism to grant or change record-level access; it cannot replicate sharing inheritance. B is incorrect: \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" and implicit parent-based sharing are properties of master-detail relationships, not standard lookups, so this claim misapplies that inheritance behavior. C is incorrect: Public Read/Write grants universal access regardless of Account ownership, which is a fundamentally different (and less controlled) visibility model than true parent-based inheritance.,")
        Question(id: "55", question: "A wealth management firm needs Financial Account records visible only to the assigned advisor and their direct manager in the role hierarchy, never to unrelated advisors, even though OWD must remain Private for compliance. Which combination of features satisfies this without excessive custom code?", options: [("A", "Private OWD combined with the standard role hierarchy (assuming advisors report to managers in the hierarchy) which grants managers automatic visibility into their direct reports' records, requiring no additional sharing rules for the manager relationship."), ("B", "Private OWD combined with Public Groups containing every advisor and manager in the company, granting all of them equal visibility to every Financial Account."), ("C", "Public Read Only OWD, since this is required to allow any manager visibility into subordinate records at all."), ("D", "Private OWD with \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" disabled, since disabling it is required for managers to see subordinate records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "With Private OWD and role hierarchy enabled (the default, \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" checked), managers automatically inherit visibility into records owned by their subordinates without needing extra sharing rules, precisely matching the stated requirement. B is incorrect: A single public group containing every advisor and manager would grant universal visibility across all Financial Accounts, violating the requirement that unrelated advisors never see each other's records. C is incorrect: Public Read Only would expose every record to every user in the org, far exceeding the requirement and violating compliance intent. D is incorrect: Disabling \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" removes the automatic manager-to-subordinate visibility that this scenario depends on; it should remain enabled, not disabled.,")
        Question(id: "56", question: "An architect must expose invoice data stored in a legacy AS/400 mainframe inside Salesforce reports without persisting the data, since the volume (500 million rows) would exceed feasible storage limits. What virtualization limitation should be raised as a key risk?", options: [("A", "External objects automatically cache all 500 million rows locally in Salesforce the first time they're queried, defeating the storage-avoidance goal."), ("B", "External objects support unlimited SOSL full-text search across all fields, making reporting fully equivalent to standard objects."), ("C", "External objects accessed via Salesforce Connect have restricted SOQL/SOSL support and cannot be used directly in standard Salesforce reports with full aggregate functionality the way persisted custom objects can, requiring careful validation of which report types and filters are actually supported."), ("D", "External objects cannot be related to standard Salesforce objects like Account under any circumstances, making integration with existing CRM data impossible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A core limitation of external objects is reduced reporting and query capability (no SOSL, limited SOQL) compared to standard/custom objects, which directly affects whether the required reporting use case is even fully achievable and must be validated early. A is incorrect: External objects do not cache full datasets locally; they query the external source live, which is exactly why storage isn't consumed, but it also means no such automatic full caching occurs. B is incorrect: SOSL is explicitly not supported against external objects, the opposite of what this option claims. D is incorrect: External objects can be related to standard Salesforce objects (e.g., via indirect lookup or external lookup relationship fields), so this blanket claim of impossibility is incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "57", question: "A hospital network wants a single Patient view spanning three legacy Salesforce orgs created after separate regional acquisitions. Leadership wants the fastest path to a unified view without a multi-year full consolidation project. What should the architect propose as an interim architecture, and what is the trade-off?", options: [("A", "A federated approach using Salesforce Connect (Cross-Org Adapter) or middleware-based real-time API calls to surface each region's Patient data within a chosen \"primary\" org, trading off some latency and dependency on all orgs' uptime for a materially faster time-to-value than full consolidation."), ("B", "Immediate full data migration and org consolidation completed within a single weekend, since Salesforce provides an automated one-click org-merge tool."), ("C", "Ignore the unified view requirement until a full multi-year consolidation project can be resourced and completed."), ("D", "Manually re-key patient data into a fourth, brand-new org via spreadsheet exports on a weekly basis.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Federating live access across the three orgs (via Cross-Org Adapter or middleware) directly addresses the stated goal of a fast interim unified view without undertaking the multi-year consolidation project, at the honest cost of latency and dependency on all systems' availability. B is incorrect: There is no such automated one-click org-merge tool in Salesforce; consolidating three orgs is a substantial project involving data migration, deduplication, and reconciliation. C is incorrect: Deferring entirely until a multi-year project completes fails to meet leadership's explicit ask for a faster interim solution. D is incorrect: Manual weekly spreadsheet re-keying is error-prone, stale, and not a scalable or real \"unified view\" solution for a hospital network's patient data.,")
        Question(id: "58", question: "What licensing and object-access consideration should an architect flag when 1,000 Platform-licensed internal users need to view (but not edit) standard Opportunity records for context, even though their day-to-day work is entirely on custom objects?", options: [("A", "Platform licenses include full read/write access to all standard objects including Opportunity by default, at no additional cost."), ("B", "Salesforce Platform licenses do not include access to certain standard CRM objects like Opportunity, so the architect must either upgrade the affected users to a CRM-inclusive license or design an alternative (e.g., a custom object mirroring the needed Opportunity fields) if upgrading all 1,000 users is cost-prohibitive."), ("C", "Platform license users can be granted Opportunity access simply by adjusting field-level security, with no object-level licensing restriction involved."), ("D", "Only System Administrator profile users are restricted from standard object access under Platform licenses; regular Platform users have full access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Platform licenses are explicitly scoped to exclude access to certain standard CRM objects such as Opportunity, which is a licensing-level restriction (not just a permission setting), forcing the architect to weigh license upgrade costs against a data-model workaround. A is incorrect: Platform licenses specifically exclude standard CRM objects like Opportunity from their license entitlements; this claim of full default access is incorrect. C is incorrect: Field-level security only controls field visibility for users who already have object-level access; it cannot grant access to an object the license itself does not entitle. D is incorrect: The Opportunity access restriction under Platform licenses applies to the license type itself, not selectively to System Administrators versus other users.,")
        Question(id: "59", question: "A customer wants a lightweight way to expose read-only summarized Order data from an external e-commerce platform inside Salesforce Opportunity records, refreshed at most once per day, without ongoing real-time callouts. Which approaches fit this requirement well? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "A Big Object updated only through manual CSV uploads performed by a business user each morning, since Big Objects cannot be populated any other way."), ("B", "A Visualforce page that performs a live SOAP callout to the e-commerce platform every time any user opens any Opportunity record, regardless of refresh tolerance."), ("C", "Salesforce Connect external objects configured for real-time synchronous callouts on every page load, since this is required even when daily refresh is acceptable."), ("D", "A scheduled batch Apex or middleware job that periodically extracts and writes summarized Order data into a custom object or fields on Opportunity."), ("E", "Given the daily refresh tolerance, avoiding live external objects or real-time callouts, since the ongoing callout overhead isn't justified by the business need.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "Given a daily refresh tolerance rather than a real-time requirement, a scheduled batch sync into a persisted structure (D) is simpler and more resilient, and deliberately avoiding live callouts (E) is appropriate since the ongoing overhead isn't justified by the stated business need. C is incorrect: Real-time synchronous callouts on every page load introduce latency and external dependency risk that isn't justified when the business only needs data refreshed once daily. D is incorrect: Big Objects can be populated via Apex, Bulk API, or CSV, not exclusively manual CSV upload; this claim overly restricts valid ingestion methods. E is incorrect: A live SOAP callout on every page view is unnecessary overhead and risk given the explicitly stated once-daily refresh tolerance.,")
        Question(id: "60", question: "A nonprofit needs donor records visible to Regional Directors for their region only, but a small Compliance team must see all donor records nationally regardless of region, without duplicating donor data per region. What sharing design should be recommended?", options: [("A", "Create a separate \"Compliance Donor\" custom object that duplicates every donor record specifically for the Compliance team's use."), ("B", "Public Read/Write OWD for all users, since this is the only way Compliance can see every record nationally."), ("C", "Private OWD with role-hierarchy-based regional visibility for Regional Directors (each managing their region's users) plus a separate sharing rule granting the Compliance public group Read access to all donor records regardless of owner or region."), ("D", "Private OWD with no sharing rules, relying solely on manual record-by-record sharing performed by each Regional Director for every Compliance request.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Combining role-hierarchy-based regional visibility with an explicit sharing rule granting Compliance broad Read access is the standard, scalable pattern for layering a cross-cutting oversight role on top of otherwise regionally-scoped record access, without any data duplication. A is incorrect: Duplicating donor records into a second object for Compliance creates data integrity risk and directly contradicts the \"without duplicating donor data\" requirement. B is incorrect: Public Read/Write would expose every donor record to every Regional Director across all regions, violating the requirement that directors see only their own region. D is incorrect: Manual record-by-record sharing for a national Compliance team does not scale and is exactly the kind of case a sharing rule is designed to solve declaratively.,")
        Question(id: "61", question: "A retail chain's Order object has grown to 40 million rows. An architect must design a data quality strategy ensuring new Orders always reference a valid, active Product without a per-record Apex callout. What should be recommended?", options: [("A", "A picklist field listing all active Product names manually maintained by an administrator every time a Product is deactivated."), ("B", "An Apex trigger that makes a synchronous callout to an external product catalog API on every Order insert to verify product validity."), ("C", "A nightly batch Apex job that deletes any Order referencing an inactive Product after the fact."), ("D", "A validation rule referencing a formula that checks a related Product's Active field via a cross-object formula, combined with a lookup filter on the Product field to only allow selection of active Products in the UI.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A lookup filter constrains valid selections declaratively in the UI, and a validation rule with a cross-object formula enforces the same constraint on any save path (API included) without requiring any callout, meeting the stated goal directly. A is incorrect: Manually maintaining a duplicate picklist of active Product names is error-prone and redundant with the Product object's own Active field. B is incorrect: A synchronous external callout on every insert to validate something already stored in Salesforce is unnecessary overhead and risk when the Product's Active flag is already available locally. C is incorrect: Deleting invalid Orders after the fact allows bad data to persist temporarily and is a reactive cleanup, not a preventive quality control.,")
        Question(id: "62", question: "Multiple integration users (API-only) load Leads into Salesforce from various marketing tools. The company wants Lead Source values restricted to a fixed, governed list, while allowing business users to occasionally propose new values through a review process. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use a formula field for Lead Source that always returns a static default value regardless of what any integration sends."), ("B", "Use a free text field for Lead Source so that any integration can write any value without restriction or governance overhead."), ("C", "Use a picklist (not free text) for Lead Source with a defined value set governed by administrators, and route requests for new values through a change-management process rather than allowing ad hoc free text from any integration."), ("D", "Use a multi-select picklist so that every integration can select as many Lead Source values as needed without any governance review.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A standard picklist enforces a governed, finite value set at the platform level while still allowing controlled expansion through an administrative change process, directly matching the requirement for consistency with a managed path for new values. A is incorrect: A static formula field would make Lead Source meaningless for reporting, since it could never reflect the actual originating source. B is incorrect: Free text removes any governance over the values entered, allowing inconsistent or junk data from any integration, which contradicts the stated goal. D is incorrect: A multi-select picklist doesn't address the requirement for a single governed source value and adds unnecessary complexity for what should be a single-value field.,")
        Question(id: "63", question: "An architect is asked whether Salesforce Data Export (the scheduled weekly/monthly export service) is sufficient as the sole backup and export mechanism for a company's compliance needs. What limitation should be raised?", options: [("A", "Scheduled Data Export automatically encrypts all exported files using the org's Shield Platform Encryption tenant secret with no extra configuration."), ("B", "Scheduled Data Export supports arbitrary custom real-time triggers, so it can produce an export within seconds of any record change."), ("C", "Scheduled Data Export is only available for Sandbox orgs and cannot be scheduled in Production at all."), ("D", "Scheduled Data Export produces full CSV exports of most data on a limited schedule (weekly at most) and doesn't include some file/attachment content in the same package by default, so time-sensitive or complete-content compliance needs may require supplementing it with API-based or Bulk API exports.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The native scheduled export service runs at most weekly and has known gaps around certain content types, so compliance programs with tighter recency or completeness requirements typically need to supplement it with more frequent, targeted API or Bulk API exports. A is incorrect: The export service does not automatically apply Shield Platform Encryption to exported files; encryption of exported data must be handled separately by the receiving process. B is incorrect: Scheduled Data Export runs on a fixed weekly/monthly schedule, not in response to real-time triggers; it cannot produce near-instant post-change exports. C is incorrect: Scheduled Data Export is available in Production orgs (subject to edition) and is not restricted to Sandbox environments.,")
        Question(id: "64", question: "What governance and security consideration should be raised when a Salesforce admin plans to export data using Data Loader from an org where Shield Platform Encryption protects several fields, and field-level security restricts those fields for most profiles?", options: [("A", "Field-level security is bypassed entirely by Data Loader exports, exposing all field values to any user who has API access enabled."), ("B", "Shield Platform Encryption automatically decrypts all fields for any user running Data Loader regardless of their permission set, since Data Loader operates with system-level access by default."), ("C", "Only users whose profile/permission set grants both View Encrypted Data and field-level read access will see plaintext values in the export; other exporting users will see masked or blank values for those fields, so the exporting user's permissions directly determine what actually appears in the exported file."), ("D", "Encrypted fields cannot be exported at all under any circumstances, making Data Loader unusable for objects containing any Shield-encrypted field.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Data Loader respects the running user's permissions, so encrypted field visibility in an export is gated by both the \"View Encrypted Data\" permission and standard field-level security, meaning the export output varies based on who runs it, which is exactly the governance nuance to flag. A is incorrect: Field-level security is enforced for Data Loader exports just as it is for other API-based access; it is not bypassed simply because API access is enabled. B is incorrect: Data Loader runs as the authenticated user, not with elevated system access, so it does not bypass encryption or permission restrictions. D is incorrect: Encrypted fields can be exported and will decrypt correctly for authorized users; they are not universally blocked from export.,")
        Question(id: "65", question: "How does exporting Shield Platform Encryption-protected data affect downstream usability once the exported file leaves Salesforce, and what should the architect flag to stakeholders?", options: [("A", "Salesforce transparently re-encrypts any exported CSV file using the org's tenant secret, so no additional data handling controls are needed after export."), ("B", "Exported files automatically retain Shield Platform Encryption protection indefinitely, requiring the same View Encrypted Data permission to open the CSV file itself."), ("C", "Once decrypted values are exported by an authorized user, the resulting file is plaintext and Salesforce's encryption no longer protects it; downstream storage, transmission, and access controls for that exported file become the organization's responsibility."), ("D", "Downstream systems can decrypt Shield-protected exports automatically as long as they are also Salesforce orgs, with no separate key exchange required.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Shield Platform Encryption protects data at rest within Salesforce; once a user with sufficient permission exports decrypted values, the resulting file carries no inherent Salesforce protection, so the organization must apply its own downstream controls (secure storage, access restriction, transmission encryption) for that exported data. A is incorrect: There is no automatic re-encryption of exported files by Salesforce; protecting the file after export is entirely the organization's responsibility. B is incorrect: Exported CSV files are ordinary files with no built-in Salesforce encryption enforcement; opening them requires no special Salesforce permission once they've left the platform. D is incorrect: There's no automatic cross-org decryption mechanism tied to Shield; any downstream system receiving the data works with whatever was actually exported (plaintext, in this case).,")
        Question(id: "66", question: "A financial services firm wants to periodically export ContentDocument (file) data attached to Cases for a compliance archive, in addition to standard object records. What should the architect account for in this export design? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "ContentDocument records automatically include full binary content in any standard SOQL query without additional considerations."), ("B", "File content is retrieved separately from record data, typically via the ContentVersion object's VersionData field through the API, rather than being included automatically in a standard object CSV export."), ("C", "Binary file content counts against API response size and governor limits differently than simple field data, often requiring chunked or streamed retrieval for large files."), ("D", "Case records and their attached files are always stored in the same underlying object, so no separate query is ever needed for the file content."), ("E", "File storage and data storage share the exact same per-user allocation, so exporting files has no separate storage implication to consider.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Retrieving file binaries requires a distinct call to ContentVersion's VersionData (not a plain object export), and because binary payloads are larger and handled differently than field data, response size and governor limit considerations require chunked or specialized retrieval for a compliance-grade export. A is incorrect: Standard SOQL queries against ContentDocument/ContentVersion return metadata; retrieving the actual binary content requires a specific call to the VersionData field, not an ordinary field selection. D is incorrect: Files are stored as separate ContentDocument/ContentVersion records linked via ContentDocumentLink, not embedded directly inside the Case record, so a separate query path is required. E is incorrect: File storage and data storage are tracked as separate allocations in Salesforce, not a shared single limit, which matters for planning the export and ongoing storage.,")
        Question(id: "67", question: "A EU-based customer requires PII fields to be identifiable, exportable on demand for a data subject access request, and eventually erasable while preserving referential integrity with related audit records. What combination of approaches should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Store all PII in a single unstructured \"Notes\" text field so it can be manually redacted with find-and-replace when a request arrives."), ("B", "Hard-delete the Contact record entirely whenever a data subject request is received, regardless of any related audit trail dependencies."), ("C", "Rely solely on Field-Level Security to restrict PII visibility, treating this as fully equivalent to satisfying GDPR erasure and portability obligations."), ("D", "Field-level Data Classification to identify PII fields, a documented export/query process (or Privacy Center) to fulfill access requests, and an anonymization approach (nulling/masking identifying fields while retaining the record shell) rather than hard deletion, to preserve referential integrity with dependent audit records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Classifying PII fields enables systematic identification, a defined export process supports portability requests, and anonymizing rather than hard-deleting preserves referential integrity with related audit/history records while still honoring the substance of an erasure request. A is incorrect: Storing PII in a single unstructured field makes systematic identification, targeted export, and selective erasure far harder than using discrete, classified fields. B is incorrect: Hard-deleting the Contact can break referential integrity with related audit trail records that must be retained, and may not be the appropriate technique when anonymization can satisfy the same erasure intent. C is incorrect: Field-Level Security controls who can view a field but does nothing to support export/portability or erasure obligations, so it cannot stand in for those distinct GDPR requirements.,")
        Question(id: "68", question: "What is the key difference between Shield Platform Encryption and Classic Encryption that should drive the choice for protecting a Social Security Number field used in list view filters and reports?", options: [("A", "Shield Platform Encryption supports case-sensitive/insensitive exact-match filtering and some reporting via deterministic encryption schemes on certain field types, while Classic Encryption offers weaker functional support and cannot be used in most filters or reports at all."), ("B", "Classic Encryption and Shield Platform Encryption are functionally identical, differing only in price, with no difference in filtering or reporting support."), ("C", "Classic Encryption supports full reporting and filtering on encrypted values, while Shield Platform Encryption blocks all filtering and reporting entirely."), ("D", "Shield Platform Encryption can only be applied to Text fields, while Classic Encryption supports all field types including picklists and dates.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Shield Platform Encryption (especially with deterministic encryption options) supports certain filtering, list view, and reporting use cases that Classic Encryption, a much more limited legacy masking feature, does not support at all, making Shield the appropriate recommendation here. B is incorrect: The two features differ substantially in supported functionality (filtering, reporting, field type coverage), not just price; treating them as identical is incorrect. C is incorrect: This reverses the actual capability gap; Classic Encryption is the more restrictive legacy feature with far less filter/report support than Shield. D is incorrect: Shield Platform Encryption supports a broader range of field types than Classic Encryption, not a narrower one as this option claims.,")
        Question(id: "69", question: "A customer wants to apply Shield Platform Encryption broadly across many fields used heavily in existing SOQL WHERE clauses and list view filters, expecting no functional change. What should the architect flag as a risk?", options: [("A", "Shield Platform Encryption automatically rewrites all existing SOQL queries in the org to remain compatible, requiring no manual review."), ("B", "Shield Platform Encryption has zero effect on any filter, list view, or report, so this concern can be dismissed without further analysis."), ("C", "Encrypting a field can change or restrict which filter operations remain usable (depending on encryption scheme), potentially breaking existing SOQL filters, list views, and reports that rely on non-deterministic encryption behavior, so a functional audit of existing filters is required before broad rollout."), ("D", "Only Apex-based filters are affected by Shield Platform Encryption; declarative list views and reports are always unaffected.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Depending on the encryption scheme used (probabilistic vs. deterministic), encrypting a field can limit which operators and features (like certain filters, sorting, or exact-match search) continue to work, making a pre-rollout audit of existing filter/report dependencies essential. A is incorrect: There is no automatic rewriting of existing SOQL queries by Salesforce when encryption is applied; any broken filters must be identified and fixed manually. B is incorrect: Encryption can materially affect filtering and reporting capability depending on scheme chosen, so dismissing the concern outright is inaccurate. D is incorrect: Both Apex-based and declarative (list view, report) filters can be affected by encryption; the impact isn't limited to just Apex.,")
        Question(id: "70", question: "How does Shield Platform Encryption's tenant secret and key derivation model affect an architect's disaster recovery and key rotation planning?", options: [("A", "The org's tenant secret is combined with a Salesforce master secret to derive data encryption keys, so architects must plan key rotation carefully (since old keys must be retained to decrypt historical data unless a full re-encryption is run) and understand that destroying the tenant secret without backup renders protected data permanently unrecoverable."), ("B", "Tenant secrets are stored entirely outside Salesforce's control by the customer, requiring the customer to submit them manually on every login."), ("C", "Key rotation automatically re-encrypts all historical data instantly and synchronously with zero processing time or backend job required."), ("D", "There is no way to rotate or manage encryption keys in Shield; the same key is used permanently for the lifetime of the org.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Understanding that keys derive from a tenant secret plus Salesforce's master secret, that old keys remain necessary for historical data unless a re-encryption job runs, and that a destroyed/unrecoverable tenant secret means permanent data loss are exactly the disaster recovery and rotation implications an architect must plan around. B is incorrect: Salesforce manages the tenant secret's use internally; it is not something a customer manually submits at every login, which would be operationally unworkable. C is incorrect: Re-encrypting historical data after key rotation is a backend process that takes time (often via a scheduled job), not an instantaneous, zero-cost operation. D is incorrect: Shield Platform Encryption does support key rotation and management (including generating new tenant secrets); the claim that keys can never be rotated is incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "71", question: "A company's data governance program wants to formally track which business unit owns data quality for the Account object and enforce quality thresholds over time. Which components should the architect recommend combining? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "A designated data steward (or stewardship group) for the Account domain, accountable for data quality outcomes."), ("B", "Relying exclusively on validation rules, since data stewardship and business ownership are unnecessary once validation rules exist."), ("C", "Measurable data quality metrics (e.g., percent of Accounts missing required firmographic fields) tracked via reports/dashboards reviewed on a regular governance cadence."), ("D", "Assigning data stewardship to whichever user happens to create the most Account records that quarter, recalculated automatically without any formal committee."), ("E", "Avoiding any specific owner for data quality, since centralizing ownership discourages broad participation in data entry quality.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Formal data governance combines clear business ownership (A) with measurable, tracked quality metrics reviewed on a cadence (C), giving the organization both accountability and visibility into whether quality goals are being met over time. B is incorrect: Validation rules enforce entry-time correctness but say nothing about ongoing measurement, trending, or business accountability for data quality, which stewardship and metrics provide. D is incorrect: Rotating stewardship based on record creation volume provides no consistent accountability and ignores the deliberate, role-based nature of data stewardship. E is incorrect: Lack of clear ownership is a common root cause of data quality decay; deliberately avoiding ownership contradicts standard governance practice.,")
        Question(id: "72", question: "What is a key difference between a centralized data governance model (single governance board) and a federated model (domain-level stewards) that an architect should raise when advising a multi-business-unit conglomerate implementing Salesforce?", options: [("A", "A federated model requires every business unit to use a completely separate Salesforce org, since shared orgs cannot support federated governance."), ("B", "Centralized and federated governance models are functionally identical in practice, differing only in the org chart title given to the governance lead."), ("C", "A centralized model offers more consistent standards but can become a bottleneck across many business units with different needs, while a federated model scales decision-making to domain experts at the cost of potential inconsistency between units unless cross-cutting standards are still enforced centrally."), ("D", "A centralized model cannot enforce any data quality standard that a federated model can, making centralization strictly inferior in all cases.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "This is the standard governance trade-off: centralization yields consistency but risks becoming a bottleneck at scale across diverse business units, while federation distributes decisions to domain stewards for speed and relevance at some risk of divergence, unless minimum cross-cutting standards are still centrally enforced. A is incorrect: Federated governance is an organizational/process model and does not require separate Salesforce orgs per business unit; it can operate within a single shared org. B is incorrect: The two models differ meaningfully in decision-making speed, consistency, and scalability, not merely in title; treating them as identical misses the actual trade-off. D is incorrect: Centralized governance is not strictly inferior; it typically enforces more consistent standards than a federated model, which is its core advantage, not a weakness.,")
        Question(id: "73", question: "How does Salesforce Shield Event Monitoring support a data governance program's need to know who accessed sensitive Account records, and what is a limitation compared to a full external SIEM integration?", options: [("A", "Event Monitoring only tracks changes made through Data Loader and has no visibility into UI-based or API-based access."), ("B", "Event Monitoring automatically blocks any unauthorized access attempt in real time, functioning as a complete substitute for a SIEM with zero configuration."), ("C", "Event Monitoring captures detailed platform events (e.g., report exports, API calls, login events) that can be reviewed or streamed for analysis, but on its own it lacks the long-term correlation, alerting rules, and cross-system context that a dedicated external SIEM platform provides."), ("D", "Event Monitoring data cannot be exported or streamed anywhere outside of Salesforce Setup, limiting it to manual review only within the Setup UI.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Event Monitoring provides the detailed access/event data feed needed to answer \"who did what and when,\" but real governance programs typically stream this into a SIEM for long-term correlation, custom alerting, and cross-system context that Event Monitoring alone does not provide. A is incorrect: Event Monitoring covers a broad range of event types including logins, API calls, and report exports, not just Data Loader activity. B is incorrect: Event Monitoring is a monitoring and logging capability, not an active blocking mechanism; it does not itself prevent unauthorized access in real time. D is incorrect: Event Monitoring data can be accessed via API and streamed (e.g., via Event Monitoring Analytics or third-party integration) to external systems, contradicting the claim that it's Setup-UI-only.,")
        Question(id: "74", question: "A customer must retain financial records for 7 years per regulation but also has an active legal hold on a subset of records related to ongoing litigation that could extend indefinitely. How should the architect design automated retention/purging to respect both requirements?", options: [("A", "Disable all retention automation entirely for the whole object whenever any single record is under legal hold, to avoid any risk of accidental deletion."), ("B", "Purge all records older than 7 years on a fixed schedule with no exceptions, since legal holds cannot be represented in Salesforce automation criteria."), ("C", "Implement retention automation (e.g., scheduled Flow/Apex batch or Privacy Center retention policy) that purges records once they exceed the 7-year threshold, but explicitly exclude any record flagged with an active legal hold indicator from the purge criteria until the hold is lifted."), ("D", "Manually track legal holds in a spreadsheet outside Salesforce, since flagging holds within Salesforce records is not a supported governance pattern.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A legal hold flag stored on or related to the record can be explicitly checked by retention automation, allowing the standard 7-year purge to apply broadly while excluding held records specifically, which is exactly the kind of conditional logic Flow/batch Apex/Privacy Center policies can express. A is incorrect: Disabling retention automation for the entire object due to one held record would leave many unrelated, eligible records improperly retained (or purged) rather than being handled individually. B is incorrect: Legal holds can absolutely be represented as a field/flag and checked within automation criteria; claiming otherwise ignores standard, supported design patterns. D is incorrect: Tracking holds outside Salesforce disconnects the flag from the very automation that needs to check it, making reliable enforcement much harder than a native field-based approach.,")
        Question(id: "75", question: "What governance considerations are specific to CCPA (California Consumer Privacy Act) compliance in Salesforce that differ from a typical GDPR-focused implementation? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "CCPA and GDPR have identical legal requirements, so any GDPR-compliant Salesforce design automatically satisfies CCPA obligations with no additional configuration."), ("B", "CCPA requires supporting a consumer's right to opt out of the sale/sharing of their personal information, requiring a distinct consent/preference flag and process separate from GDPR's general consent and erasure mechanics."), ("C", "CCPA only applies to Salesforce orgs physically hosted in California data centers, making org location the sole determining compliance factor."), ("D", "CCPA requires maintaining a data inventory of what personal information is collected and to whom it may be disclosed."), ("E", "CCPA prohibits any use of Shield Platform Encryption, requiring all California-related PII to remain unencrypted for consumer inspection purposes.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "The \"opt-out of sale/sharing\" right (B) is a CCPA-specific consumer right without a direct GDPR equivalent, and the broader data inventory obligation (D) is a distinct requirement governance design must specifically account for beyond standard GDPR consent handling. A is incorrect: While there is overlap, CCPA introduces requirements (like opt-out of sale) not present in GDPR, so a GDPR-only design does not automatically cover CCPA obligations. C is incorrect: CCPA applicability is determined by factors like where the business operates and whose data is processed, not by the physical location of the Salesforce data center hosting the org. E is incorrect: CCPA does not prohibit encryption; encryption is actually a recommended safeguard for protecting personal information, not something the regulation bans.,")
        Question(id: "76", question: "A manufacturing company wants to formally govern how object schema changes (new fields, modified picklists, deprecated fields) are proposed, reviewed, and tracked across quarterly releases as part of its metadata governance practice. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Establish a change-management process using version-controlled metadata (e.g., tracked in a source repository via a CI/CD pipeline) with a review/approval step before deployment, giving the organization an auditable history of schema evolution over time."), ("B", "Allow any System Administrator to modify object schema directly in Production at any time with no review process, since Salesforce's own audit trail feature makes formal change management redundant."), ("C", "Require that schema changes only be discussed verbally in team meetings, with no written record, to keep the governance process lightweight."), ("D", "Freeze all schema changes permanently after the initial go-live, treating any future business need as out of scope for the data model.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Version-controlling metadata and requiring a review/approval gate before deployment creates both a governance checkpoint and a durable audit trail of schema evolution, directly meeting the stated need for a formal, trackable change process across releases. B is incorrect: Unrestricted direct Production changes with no review process removes exactly the control and traceability the company is asking to establish; Setup Audit Trail alone doesn't provide pre-change review. C is incorrect: A purely verbal process leaves no durable record for future impact analysis or audits, undermining the stated governance goal. D is incorrect: Freezing schema changes indefinitely is impractical for any evolving business and isn't a real governance practice; the goal is controlled change, not no change.,")
        Question(id: "77", question: "How should data classification metadata and Shield Field Audit Trail be layered together in a GDPR-relevant data model to support both proactive governance and historical compliance evidence?", options: [("A", "Data Classification and Field Audit Trail are mutually exclusive features; enabling one automatically disables the other on the same object."), ("B", "Data Classification tags identify which fields are sensitive so governance processes and tooling know where to focus, while Field Audit Trail provides the actual historical record of changes to those (and other) fields over a longer retention window than standard Field History Tracking, giving auditors both the \"what's sensitive\" map and the \"what changed and when\" evidence."), ("C", "Field Audit Trail automatically applies Data Classification labels to every tracked field without any manual tagging required."), ("D", "Data Classification metadata retroactively generates historical change records for fields that were never previously tracked, eliminating the need to enable Field Audit Trail at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The two features serve complementary purposes — classification is a labeling/discovery layer identifying sensitive fields, while Field Audit Trail is the actual change-history retention mechanism — and combining them gives governance both the map of what matters and the long-term evidentiary trail auditors need. A is incorrect: The two features are independent and can both be enabled on the same object simultaneously; there is no mutual exclusivity between them. C is incorrect: Field Audit Trail tracks historical values for fields it's configured to monitor; it does not automatically assign classification labels, which remain a separate manual metadata step. D is incorrect: Field Audit Trail only begins capturing history from the point it is enabled forward; it cannot retroactively create historical records for changes that occurred before it was turned on.,")
        Question(id: "78", question: "A consent management model must let a Contact's marketing consent preference be captured once in Salesforce and reliably propagate to a connected email marketing platform and a separate SMS platform. What data model and integration approach should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Store consent preferences only as free-text notes on the Contact record, since downstream systems can parse natural language reliably without structured data."), ("B", "Store consent as a structured field (or related consent object with type, timestamp, and source) on Contact, and use an event-driven integration (e.g., Platform Events or Change Data Capture) to notify downstream marketing and SMS platforms whenever the consent value changes, rather than relying on periodic manual exports."), ("C", "Require the downstream marketing and SMS platforms to independently ask each contact for consent again, since consent captured in Salesforce cannot be technically propagated to other systems."), ("D", "Store consent as a single global custom setting shared by all Contacts, since consent preferences are the same for every customer in the org.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A structured, timestamped consent field or object gives downstream systems something reliably parseable, and event-driven propagation (Platform Events/CDC) ensures preference changes reach connected marketing/SMS platforms promptly and consistently, rather than depending on fragile batch exports. A is incorrect: Free-text notes are not reliably machine-parseable, creating a high risk of misinterpreting or missing a consent change when propagating to downstream systems. C is incorrect: Consent captured in Salesforce absolutely can be integrated out to other systems via APIs or events; claiming it's technically impossible is incorrect and ignores the point of the requirement. D is incorrect: Consent is inherently a per-Contact preference; storing it as one shared org-wide value would incorrectly apply a single preference to every customer.,")
        Question(id: "79", question: "A custom object has 80 million records. A frequently run report filters on a custom picklist field with only 3 possible values, each roughly a third of all records. What should the architect flag about this filter's selectivity?", options: [("A", "Any indexed field is automatically selective regardless of how many rows match, so adding a custom index guarantees fast performance here."), ("B", "A filter matching roughly a third of 80 million rows is not selective (well above the \\~10-30% selectivity threshold), so the query optimizer likely won't use a custom index efficiently and may fall back to a full table scan regardless of indexing."), ("C", "Picklist fields are always excluded from selectivity calculations entirely, so this filter's performance cannot be evaluated using the Query Plan tool."), ("D", "Selectivity thresholds only apply to Lookup relationship fields, never to picklist or text fields, making this filter's design irrelevant to LDV performance.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "With each picklist value matching roughly 33% of 80 million rows, the filter falls well outside the standard selectivity thresholds (roughly under 10% for a single condition, up to \\~30% combined), so even a custom index on that field likely won't be chosen by the optimizer, resulting in a full or near-full table scan. A is incorrect: Selectivity depends on the proportion of matching rows, not merely on whether an index exists; a non-selective indexed field can still be skipped by the optimizer in favor of a table scan. C is incorrect: Picklist fields are fully subject to selectivity analysis like any other field type; there's no special exclusion for picklists in the Query Plan tool. D is incorrect: Selectivity thresholds apply broadly to indexed fields of many types, not exclusively to lookup relationships; this claim incorrectly narrows the concept.,")
        Question(id: "80", question: "What are the standard selectivity thresholds an architect should reference when evaluating whether a custom index will actually be used by Salesforce's query optimizer for a single-field filter versus a combined multi-field filter?", options: [("A", "Roughly under 10% of total records matching for a single selective filter, and up to about 30% when combining multiple filtered fields together, are the general thresholds guiding whether the optimizer treats a query as selective enough to use an index."), ("B", "Any filter matching fewer than 1 million absolute records is automatically considered selective, regardless of what percentage of the total object that represents."), ("C", "Selectivity thresholds are fixed at exactly 50% for all filter types and cannot vary based on single versus combined field conditions."), ("D", "There are no defined selectivity thresholds; the optimizer decision is entirely random and cannot be reasoned about by architects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The commonly referenced Salesforce selectivity guidance uses roughly 10% for a single filtered field and roughly 30% for combined multi-field selectivity as the thresholds below which the optimizer is likely to use an index rather than perform a full table scan. B is incorrect: Selectivity is evaluated as a percentage of total object rows, not as a fixed absolute record count threshold like 1 million. C is incorrect: The thresholds differ meaningfully between single-field (\\~10%) and combined multi-field (\\~30%) scenarios; a single fixed 50% figure misrepresents the actual guidance. D is incorrect: The optimizer's behavior is deterministic and based on statistics and defined thresholds, not random; architects can and do reason about it using the Query Plan tool.,")
        Question(id: "81", question: "A SOQL query on a 60-million-row object filters on a custom Date field that is frequently null for about 40% of records, using a WHERE clause of Field \\!= null. What should the architect anticipate?", options: [("A", "Fields that can contain nulls are permanently excluded from ever being marked as custom indexes, regardless of query pattern."), ("B", "Null values are always indexed identically to non-null values, so this filter will automatically be as selective as any other value-based filter."), ("C", "Salesforce automatically rewrites any \"\\!= null\" filter into an indexed range scan with no further optimization needed."), ("D", "Null values are not stored in the standard index, so a filter effectively checking for non-null values on a field with a large proportion of nulls can still behave inefficiently, and the architect should consider a workaround such as a formula/checkbox flag field that is always populated to make the filter selective and indexable.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Because null values aren't stored in the standard index, filtering for non-null values doesn't get the same indexed lookup benefit as filtering for a specific present value; a common architectural workaround is a separate always-populated flag field (e.g., a checkbox) that can be indexed and filtered on instead. A is incorrect: A field capable of containing nulls can still be marked as a custom index; the limitation is about null values specifically not being indexed, not a blanket exclusion of the field from indexing altogether. B is incorrect: Null values specifically are excluded from the index, which is the root cause of the performance concern, not indexed identically to non-null values. C is incorrect: There's no automatic query rewriting of \"\\!= null\" filters into an optimized indexed scan; the underlying null-indexing limitation persists regardless of query phrasing.,")
        Question(id: "82", question: "Using the Salesforce Query Plan tool on a slow report against a 90-million-row object, the architect sees a plan with a very high \"Cost\" value and an operation labeled as a table scan (\"TableScan\") rather than an index-based operation. What should this tell the architect?", options: [("A", "A TableScan operation with high Cost actually indicates optimal performance, since TableScan is the fastest available access path for any LDV object."), ("B", "The query is not leveraging any selective index and is likely scanning a large portion of the object's records, indicating the filters used are not selective enough or lack an appropriate custom index, and redesigning the filter criteria or adding a targeted index should be considered."), ("C", "The Query Plan tool only reports on Apex-triggered queries and has no visibility into report-generated SOQL, so this result must be from unrelated backend activity."), ("D", "A high Cost value with a TableScan always means the object has exceeded its storage limit and must be archived immediately before any further queries can run.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A TableScan with a high relative Cost is the Query Plan tool's signal that the optimizer could not use a selective index for this query, directly indicating a need to revisit filter selectivity or add/adjust an index rather than accept the current performance. A is incorrect: A TableScan is generally the least efficient access path at large volume, the opposite of optimal; the tool flags it as something to investigate, not celebrate. C is incorrect: The Query Plan tool can be used to analyze the SOQL underlying various operations, including report-generated queries, not solely Apex-triggered ones. D is incorrect: A TableScan result is unrelated to whether the object has hit a storage limit; it reflects query execution strategy, not storage capacity status.,")
        Question(id: "83", question: "An LDV object has a formula field referencing a related object's fields, and reports filtering on this formula field are timing out at 150 million records. Which statements accurately describe the problem and an appropriate fix? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Increasing the formula field's decimal precision setting directly improves formula field query performance at scale."), ("B", "Adding the formula field to a skinny table automatically materializes and indexes all formula field values with no further configuration."), ("C", "Converting the formula field into a roll-up summary field is a general substitute, since roll-up summary fields always outperform any persisted custom field for filtering purposes."), ("D", "Formula fields are calculated at query/view time and cannot be indexed for filtering the way a persisted field can, which is the root cause of the timeout."), ("E", "Replacing the formula field with a persisted (stored) field populated via a trigger, Flow, or batch process whenever the source data changes allows indexing and dramatically better filter performance.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "The timeout stems directly from formula fields being computed on the fly and non-indexable (D); converting to a persisted field kept in sync by automation (E) allows indexing and is the standard fix for this exact LDV filtering problem. A is incorrect: Decimal precision is a display/rounding setting and has no bearing on the fundamental performance limitation of formula fields being computed at query time. B is incorrect: Skinny tables can include certain formula fields in some cases, but they don't automatically solve this without being explicitly requested and configured by Salesforce Support, and even skinny tables have field type restrictions that must be verified first. C is incorrect: Roll-up summary fields aggregate child records into a parent field; they are not a general substitute for an arbitrary cross-object formula field and don't inherently outperform a simple persisted field.,")
        Question(id: "84", question: "An object has 200 million records, but only about 10% are marked Active via a checkbox field, and nearly all queries filter on Active \\= true. What indexing and data model strategy should the architect propose to keep active-record queries fast without harming full-object access patterns?", options: [("A", "Split the 200 million records into 20 separate custom objects of 10 million each based on an arbitrary batch number, since Salesforce cannot index any field beyond 50 million records."), ("B", "Delete all inactive records to eliminate the imbalance entirely, since retaining historical inactive records serves no possible business purpose in an LDV context."), ("C", "Convert the Active field to a formula field so it's always recalculated fresh, avoiding the need for any indexing strategy."), ("D", "Request a custom index (or work with Salesforce Support on a skinny table) on the Active checkbox field, since a 10% match rate is within selective thresholds and will let the optimizer use the index efficiently for the common active-record query pattern, while other access patterns can still use different filters as needed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "With active records being \\~10% of the total, this filter falls right at the selective threshold, making a custom index (potentially with Salesforce Support's help via a skinny table) an effective way to keep the dominant active-record query pattern fast without needing to restructure the entire object. A is incorrect: There is no such 50-million-record indexing ceiling, and splitting one logical object into 20 arbitrary objects massively complicates reporting, automation, and integration without a real technical justification. B is incorrect: Deleting inactive records ignores legitimate business or compliance reasons for retention and is a drastic, likely unacceptable data-loss action compared to an indexing solution. C is incorrect: Converting Active to a formula field removes the ability to index it at all, working directly against the goal of fast filtering.,")
        Question(id: "85", question: "What LDV-specific risk should the architect raise about using standard Salesforce reports and list views directly against a 120-million-row custom object for ad hoc, unindexed filtering by business users?", options: [("A", "Ad hoc reports and list views built on non-selective, non-indexed filter criteria are prone to timeouts or significant delays at this volume, so the architect should guide report/list view design toward indexed fields and consider pre-aggregated or summarized reporting objects for common ad hoc analysis needs."), ("B", "Reports and list views automatically bypass all indexing considerations and always execute with constant-time performance regardless of object size."), ("C", "Standard reports enforce a hard cap of exactly 2,000 rows scanned internally, guaranteeing consistent performance on any object size."), ("D", "List views cannot be created at all on any object exceeding 100 million records, making this scenario technically impossible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Business users building ad hoc reports/list views with arbitrary, potentially non-indexed filters on a 120-million-row object risk timeouts, so the practical mitigation is steering users toward indexed filter fields and offering pre-aggregated reporting structures for common analysis patterns. B is incorrect: Reports and list views are still subject to the same underlying SOQL selectivity and indexing considerations as any other query; they don't get a special performance exemption. C is incorrect: There's no such fixed 2,000-row internal scan cap guaranteeing performance; large non-selective queries can scan far more rows internally and time out. D is incorrect: List views can technically be created on very large objects; they just carry real performance risk with poor filter design, which is the actual concern to manage.,")
        Question(id: "86", question: "How does data skew (e.g., ownership skew) interact with LDV to compound sharing calculation performance issues, and what combined approach addresses both problems together?", options: [("A", "A skewed owner with millions of records on an LDV object multiplies both the base sharing recalculation cost (from skew) and the sheer computation volume (from LDV), so combined mitigation typically involves redistributing ownership where feasible plus using deferred/batched sharing recalculation and selective indexing to manage both issues together rather than treating them as separate problems."), ("B", "LDV and data skew are unrelated concerns that never compound; each must be solved with entirely independent, non-overlapping techniques."), ("C", "Data skew only matters on objects under 1 million records, so once LDV thresholds are reached, skew-related concerns become irrelevant."), ("D", "The only fix for skew combined with LDV is a full data archival project removing at least half of all records regardless of business need.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Ownership or lookup skew multiplies the cost of sharing recalculation, and doing that recalculation across an already-massive LDV dataset compounds the problem, so effective mitigation looks at both dimensions together: redistributing where possible, deferring/batching recalculation, and applying appropriate indexing. B is incorrect: Skew and LDV directly compound each other because sharing recalculation cost scales with both the concentration of ownership and the total record volume being recalculated. C is incorrect: Skew concerns are actually more, not less, impactful as record volume grows into LDV territory; there's no threshold above which skew stops mattering. D is incorrect: Archiving half of all records regardless of business need is not a targeted or appropriate fix; the actual levers are ownership redistribution, recalculation strategy, and indexing.,")
        Question(id: "87", question: "What is Async SOQL, and when is it the appropriate tool for an LDV analytics workload compared to standard synchronous SOQL?", options: [("A", "Async SOQL is designed for large-scale, non-real-time analytical queries against very large data volumes, processing results into a designated object over time rather than returning results synchronously within a single transaction, making it appropriate for big-picture analytics rather than real-time UI-driven lookups."), ("B", "Async SOQL is simply standard SOQL executed with a longer default timeout, with identical governor limits and syntax in every other respect."), ("C", "Async SOQL replaces the need for any indexing strategy since it always performs a guaranteed full-object scan efficiently regardless of data volume."), ("D", "Async SOQL can only be invoked from Lightning Web Components and is unavailable to Apex or any backend batch process.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Async SOQL is purpose-built for large-scale analytical queries over big data volumes, running asynchronously and writing results to a target object rather than blocking a transaction for a real-time answer, making it suitable for LDV analytics rather than interactive, real-time lookups. B is incorrect: Async SOQL has a distinct execution model (asynchronous, queued, writing to a results object) rather than simply being synchronous SOQL with a longer timeout. C is incorrect: Async SOQL still benefits from appropriate data organization and doesn't eliminate the value of indexing; framing it as a guaranteed efficient full scan overstates its capability. D is incorrect: Async SOQL is invoked via API/Apex-oriented mechanisms as part of backend processing, not restricted to LWC-only invocation.,")
        Question(id: "88", question: "What is a skinny table, when should an architect request one from Salesforce, and what is a key limitation to be aware of?", options: [("A", "A skinny table is a self-service Setup feature any admin can enable instantly for any object with no field type restrictions or support request required."), ("B", "A skinny table is a Salesforce-Support-provisioned, narrower copy of a subset of an object's fields (excluding certain field types like multi-select picklists and long text) kept in sync with the source table, useful for improving performance of frequently run queries/reports on LDV objects that only need a handful of fields."), ("C", "A skinny table automatically includes every field on the object, including all relationship and long text fields, functioning as a full mirror rather than a narrowed subset."), ("D", "A skinny table replaces the need for any custom index since it is always used by the optimizer regardless of query filter selectivity.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A skinny table is provisioned by Salesforce Support (not self-service), contains a narrowed set of frequently-needed fields (excluding unsupported types like multi-select picklists), and is kept in sync automatically, making it valuable specifically for speeding up narrow, frequent queries against wide, high-volume objects. A is incorrect: Skinny tables require a request to Salesforce Support to provision; they are not a self-service Setup toggle, and they do carry field type restrictions. C is incorrect: Skinny tables are deliberately narrower than the full object, specifically excluding certain unsupported field types like long text and multi-select picklists, not a full mirror. D is incorrect: The optimizer still evaluates query selectivity; a skinny table improves the underlying data access pattern for included fields but doesn't override selectivity-based optimizer decisions entirely.,")
        Question(id: "89", question: "An LDV object includes millions of file attachments (ContentVersion records) totaling several terabytes. Which statements accurately describe the distinction between data storage and file storage that should shape the archiving strategy? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Data storage and file storage are tracked as separate allocations with separate limits."), ("B", "Data storage and file storage draw from a single combined limit, so archiving either object records or files reduces the same shared allocation equally."), ("C", "A strategy purely focused on archiving object records (data storage) may still leave the org exposed to file storage limits, requiring a distinct plan for attachment content."), ("D", "File storage has no defined limit in Salesforce and can grow indefinitely without any architectural consideration."), ("E", "Attachments and files are automatically purged after 5 years by the platform with no configuration required.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Because data storage and file storage are governed by distinct limits (A), an LDV architecture addressing only record-level archiving can still run into separate file storage constraints from millions of large attachments, requiring its own dedicated strategy (C). B is incorrect: The two storage types are tracked and limited independently, not as one shared pool, which is exactly why a record-only archiving plan can miss the file storage risk. D is incorrect: File storage does have defined limits based on edition and licenses; treating it as unlimited ignores a real constraint architects must plan around. E is incorrect: There is no automatic platform-wide 5-year purge of files; retention and archival of attachments must be deliberately designed and implemented.,")
        Question(id: "90", question: "A telecom company has a Call Detail Record object exceeding 500 million rows, with 95% of queries filtering by a Customer lookup field and a date range. What combined indexing strategy should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Avoid using the Customer lookup field in queries altogether, since lookup fields are never indexed by Salesforce by default."), ("B", "Ensure the Customer lookup field (already indexed by default) is used as the leading filter, and evaluate requesting a custom composite index combining Customer and the Date field, since combining an indexed lookup with a date range filter is a common, well-supported way to achieve selective, high-performance queries on LDV objects."), ("C", "Rely exclusively on a full table scan for every query, since 500 million rows is well beyond any threshold where indexing could ever help."), ("D", "Convert the Date field to a text field to bypass any need for indexing considerations, since only Date-typed fields are subject to selectivity rules.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Lookup fields are indexed by default, and pairing that with a custom composite index including the date range field is a standard, well-supported LDV pattern for making the dominant filter combination (Customer \\+ date range) highly selective and performant. A is incorrect: Lookup fields are indexed by default in Salesforce; avoiding them contradicts this fact and would forfeit an already-available performance advantage. C is incorrect: Even at 500 million rows, selective indexed queries (matching a small percentage of records, like one customer's calls in a date range) can perform well; a blanket full table scan approach ignores this. D is incorrect: Selectivity rules apply to indexed fields broadly, not exclusively to Date-typed fields, and converting to text would not exempt the field from selectivity considerations, while losing native date range query support.,")
        Question(id: "91", question: "A customer's LDV object query filters on a custom text field using a \"LIKE '%value%'\" wildcard pattern (wildcard at both ends). What should the architect flag about this pattern's performance at 100 million records?", options: [("A", "Any wildcard filter automatically triggers Salesforce to build a temporary index on the fly for that specific query, guaranteeing fast performance."), ("B", "Leading and trailing wildcards have no effect on index usability; only trailing-only wildcards (e.g., \"value%\") ever cause performance issues."), ("C", "SOSL and SOQL LIKE wildcard behavior are identical in every respect, so switching to SOSL would provide no different performance characteristics for this pattern."), ("D", "A leading wildcard (or wildcard on both sides) prevents the standard index from being used effectively because indexes are ordered structures that rely on matching from the start of the value, so this filter will likely trigger a full table scan regardless of any index present on the field.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Standard indexes are effective for matching values from the beginning of a string; a search pattern with a leading wildcard cannot leverage that ordered structure, which is why such patterns commonly result in full table scans on large objects. A is incorrect: Salesforce does not dynamically build temporary indexes on the fly per query; index structures are defined ahead of time, not generated reactively for a single wildcard search. B is incorrect: It's specifically the leading wildcard that defeats standard index usability; a trailing-only wildcard (prefix match) can actually still leverage an index reasonably well, which is the opposite of this claim. C is incorrect: SOSL is specifically designed for more flexible text search (including substring matching) via a different underlying search index mechanism, and can behave quite differently from a SOQL LIKE with wildcards for this use case.,")
        Question(id: "92", question: "An org has a highly automation-heavy Account object (many triggers, flows, and process automations) with 50 million records, and nightly integration batch jobs are timing out due to CPU time and DML governor limit errors during bulk updates. Which architectural approach addresses the root LDV-related cause?", options: [("A", "Simply increase the batch size in the Bulk API job to 1, since smaller batches always process faster on automation-heavy objects."), ("B", "Audit and consolidate the automation (e.g., merge multiple triggers into a single well-bulkified trigger per object, review Flow entry criteria to avoid redundant executions) to reduce the cumulative per-record processing overhead multiplied across large batch volumes."), ("C", "Governor limits scale automatically with object record count, so no automation review is needed once an object surpasses LDV thresholds."), ("D", "Disable all sandbox testing for automation changes going forward, since sandbox testing itself is the primary cause of production governor limit errors.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When many separate automations each do meaningful per-record work, that overhead multiplies across a large batch; consolidating and bulkifying automation logic directly reduces the CPU/DML cost per transaction, addressing the actual root cause rather than a symptom. A is incorrect: A batch size of 1 means processing one record per transaction, which would drastically increase the number of transactions and total processing time, not improve performance. C is incorrect: Governor limits are fixed per transaction regardless of object record count; they do not scale up automatically as an object grows into LDV territory. D is incorrect: Sandbox testing is unrelated to production governor limit errors during bulk updates; this option misidentifies the cause entirely.,")
        Question(id: "93", question: "A 300-million-row object experiences degraded performance specifically during month-end when a batch job updates roughly 40% of all records in a single run, each update firing a roll-up summary recalculation on a related parent. What LDV-specific mitigation should the architect propose?", options: [("A", "Schedule the batch job to run during business hours instead of off-hours, since time of day is the primary lever for resolving this kind of performance issue."), ("B", "Permanently remove the roll-up summary field entirely, since roll-up summary fields serve no legitimate purpose at any record volume above 10 million."), ("C", "Run the entire 40% update in a single synchronous Apex transaction without batching, since batching monthly jobs is unnecessary regardless of volume."), ("D", "Consider deferring or batching the roll-up recalculation (e.g., disabling the roll-up temporarily, running updates, and recalculating via a controlled batch process afterward, or spreading the update across smaller sequential batches) rather than triggering synchronous per-record roll-up recalculation across such a large simultaneous volume.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Since roll-up recalculation is triggered synchronously by child DML, updating 40% of a 300-million-row object at once multiplies that recalculation cost dramatically; deferring/batching the recalculation strategy directly targets the actual bottleneck for this specific high-volume, concentrated update pattern. A is incorrect: Time of day doesn't change the fundamental computational cost of recalculating roll-ups across 40% of 300 million records; the actual lever is managing the recalculation strategy itself, not the clock. B is incorrect: Roll-up summary fields remain a legitimate, widely used feature at large volumes; removing them entirely sacrifices needed business functionality rather than addressing the batch timing problem. C is incorrect: A single massive synchronous transaction would almost certainly hit governor limits outright; batching is specifically recommended for large volume updates, not avoided.,")
        Question(id: "94", question: "What is the impact of index selectivity thresholds specifically on combined AND-based multi-field filters, and how should an architect design a report filtering on both a picklist (35% match rate) and a lookup field (5% match rate) together?", options: [("A", "Combining a non-selective field with a selective field always makes the entire query less selective than either field individually, guaranteeing a full table scan."), ("B", "Combined selectivity for an AND-based multi-field filter is generally more favorable than any single non-selective field alone, so even though the picklist alone (35%) isn't selective, combining it with the highly selective lookup filter (5%) can bring the effective combined selectivity within the roughly 30% combined threshold, potentially still allowing index usage on the lookup field."), ("C", "AND-based combined filters are evaluated by summing the two percentages (35% \\+ 5% \\= 40%), which always exceeds every threshold and can never use an index."), ("D", "Multi-field filter selectivity is determined solely by whichever field appears first in the WHERE clause, regardless of its individual match rate.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Combined selectivity considers how the multiple conditions interact; a highly selective lookup filter (5%) combined with a less selective picklist filter can still result in the optimizer treating the combined query as selective enough (within roughly the 30% guidance) to use the indexed lookup field effectively. A is incorrect: Combining a selective field with a non-selective one does not automatically make the whole query worse than each individually; the optimizer can still find good selectivity from the combination, particularly when one filter is quite narrow. C is incorrect: Selectivity of combined filters isn't a simple additive percentage calculation; the actual combined match rate depends on the overlap between the two conditions, not a straight sum. D is incorrect: Field order in the WHERE clause doesn't solely determine selectivity; the optimizer evaluates available indexes and statistics across the filter conditions, not just textual position.,")
        Question(id: "95", question: "A logistics company wants to archive 15 years of Shipment records (600 million+ rows) but must still support occasional exact-match lookups by Shipment ID and date for compliance audits, with query latency of a few seconds being acceptable. Storage cost must be minimized. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use Salesforce Connect to point to a live copy of the same data still held in the standard object, since virtualizing data already inside Salesforce eliminates all storage costs."), ("B", "Keep all 600 million rows in the standard custom object indefinitely, since standard object storage costs nothing extra regardless of volume once Sales Cloud licenses are purchased."), ("C", "Export all data to CSV files stored as Salesforce Files (ContentVersion) attached to a placeholder record, since Salesforce Files have no storage cost implications at any volume."), ("D", "Migrate qualifying aged Shipment records into a Big Object with a composite index leading with Shipment ID (or date, depending on dominant audit query pattern), since Big Objects are designed for exactly this profile: massive volume, infrequent access, acceptable latency, and lower storage cost than standard objects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Big Objects are purpose-built for this exact profile — very large volume, infrequent access, tolerant latency, and storage outside standard limits — and a composite index matching the audit query pattern (Shipment ID/date) keeps those occasional lookups performant. A is incorrect: Salesforce Connect virtualizes external, non-Salesforce data sources; pointing it at data that's still stored in a standard Salesforce object doesn't apply and wouldn't reduce that object's own storage footprint. B is incorrect: Standard object storage does incur cost and counts against org storage allocations; there is no free unlimited standard storage tier. C is incorrect: Salesforce Files still consume file storage allocation and have real costs at large volume; treating them as costless is incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "96", question: "What data model and indexing consideration should an architect raise when a customer requests a custom index on a multi-select picklist field for an LDV object, expecting the same performance benefit as a standard picklist index?", options: [("A", "Multi-select picklist fields have significant limitations for custom indexing and typically cannot be indexed the same way single-value fields can, so the architect should recommend an alternative design, such as breaking the values into separate boolean/checkbox fields or a related child object, if performant filtering on those values is required."), ("B", "Multi-select picklists index identically to single-select picklists with no functional difference in query performance or index behavior."), ("C", "Any field type can be indexed with identical behavior, since Salesforce applies indexing uniformly across all field types without exception."), ("D", "Multi-select picklists cannot be used in reports or dashboards at all, making the indexing question moot regardless of LDV context.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Multi-select picklists have known indexing limitations that prevent them from behaving like standard indexed fields, so when performant filtering is truly required at LDV scale, redesigning into discrete boolean fields or a related object is the recommended architectural workaround. B is incorrect: Multi-select picklists do not behave identically to single-select picklists for indexing purposes; this is precisely the limitation the architect needs to flag. C is incorrect: Field types differ meaningfully in their indexing support (e.g., long text areas and multi-select picklists have restrictions that standard picklists or lookups do not), so uniform treatment is inaccurate. D is incorrect: Multi-select picklists can appear in reports and dashboards with some limitations; they are not entirely excluded, so this overstates the restriction.,")
        Question(id: "97", question: "A 250-million-row object needs a new custom index added to support a newly introduced high-frequency query pattern. Which statements should the architect communicate to the project team about the process and timing? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Adding a custom index to an LDV object requires deleting and reloading all existing records to rebuild the table from scratch."), ("B", "Building the index on a very large existing object can take meaningful processing time, so this should be planned ahead of the go-live date for the dependent feature."), ("C", "Custom indexes are only possible on objects with fewer than 10 million records; larger objects are permanently ineligible for any additional indexing."), ("D", "Custom indexes can be self-service created instantly in Setup for any field on any object with zero backend processing time regardless of record volume."), ("E", "Custom indexes (beyond the automatically indexed standard fields) generally require submitting a request to Salesforce Support.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Requesting and building a custom index on a very large object is a Salesforce Support-mediated process (E) that takes real processing time proportional to data volume, so it needs to be planned with adequate lead time ahead of any dependent feature's launch (B). A is incorrect: Adding a custom index does not require deleting and reloading existing data; the index is built against the existing dataset without requiring a full data reload. C is incorrect: There is no such hard 10-million-record ceiling that makes larger objects permanently ineligible for additional custom indexing; LDV objects are precisely where custom indexing often matters most. D is incorrect: Custom indexes are not simply self-service instant Setup toggles; they typically require a Salesforce Support request, especially relevant at LDV scale.,")
        Question(id: "98", question: "An architect is asked whether increasing an org's total data storage limit (via purchasing additional storage) resolves the query performance problems experienced on a 400-million-row object with poorly designed filters. What should be clarified?", options: [("A", "Additional storage purchases automatically trigger Salesforce to rebuild all custom indexes for improved query performance as part of the storage upgrade process."), ("B", "Purchasing additional storage capacity only raises how much data the org can hold; it does not address query performance, which is governed by filter selectivity, indexing, and query design rather than available storage headroom."), ("C", "Query performance scales directly and proportionally with purchased storage capacity, so doubling storage doubles query speed."), ("D", "Storage limits and query performance are governed by the exact same underlying platform mechanism, making a storage increase functionally equivalent to a performance tuning exercise.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Storage capacity and query performance are separate platform concerns; buying more storage headroom does nothing to fix non-selective filters or missing indexes, which is what's actually driving the poor query performance in this scenario. A is incorrect: There's no such automatic index rebuild tied to a storage capacity purchase; indexing and storage limits are managed through entirely separate mechanisms. C is incorrect: Query speed is a function of filter selectivity and indexing strategy, not storage capacity; more storage headroom has no direct proportional effect on query speed. D is incorrect: Storage limits (a capacity constraint) and query performance (an execution efficiency concern) are distinct platform dimensions, not the same underlying mechanism.,")
        Question(id: "99", question: "A 100-million-row object needs a report showing count of records grouped by a custom picklist field, refreshed hourly for an executive dashboard. Ad hoc report grouping is timing out. What LDV-appropriate architectural alternative should be proposed?", options: [("A", "Pre-aggregate the counts on a schedule (e.g., via a nightly/hourly scheduled Flow or batch Apex job) into a small summary custom object or set of fields, and point the executive dashboard at that lightweight pre-aggregated data instead of grouping the full 100-million-row object live on each refresh."), ("B", "Increase the dashboard's refresh frequency to every 5 minutes, since more frequent refreshes always resolve report timeout issues on large objects."), ("C", "Switch the report type from Summary to Tabular, since Tabular reports are exempt from all governor limits and query timeouts regardless of object size."), ("D", "Add more columns to the report to give the query optimizer additional fields to use for automatic performance tuning.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Pre-aggregating the grouped counts on a schedule into a small summary structure avoids repeatedly grouping 100 million live rows on every dashboard refresh, trading a small amount of staleness for dramatically better and more reliable dashboard performance. B is incorrect: Increasing refresh frequency would make the underlying expensive live-grouping query run even more often, worsening rather than resolving the timeout problem. C is incorrect: Report type alone does not exempt a query from governor limits or timeouts; Tabular reports are still subject to the same underlying data volume and query performance constraints. D is incorrect: Adding more columns increases the amount of data retrieved and processed per row, which would generally make the query more expensive, not faster, and doesn't inform optimizer behavior in this way.,")
        Question(id: "100", question: "What combined data skew and LDV mitigation should be proposed for a single Account with 300,000 child Opportunities under Private OWD, where sales reps report severe lag saving any of these Opportunities?", options: [("A", "Switch the Account's OWD to Public Read/Write only for this single record, since per-record OWD overrides are a standard supported Salesforce feature."), ("B", "Consider a combination of reducing record-locking contention (e.g., avoiding concurrent bulk updates to this Account's Opportunities, using a queueing/serialization pattern for updates) and reviewing whether all 300,000 need to remain active children versus being partitioned or archived, alongside evaluating the OWD/sharing model for this specific skewed Account."), ("C", "Add a validation rule preventing any user from saving more than 1 Opportunity per day on this Account, since validation rules are the standard skew mitigation technique."), ("D", "Rename the Account to move it alphabetically outside the report sort order, since alphabetical positioning is the primary driver of record-locking skew.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "This is a textbook data skew scenario (many children under one parent) compounded by LDV concerns; the standard mitigation combines addressing lock contention patterns, reconsidering which records must remain \"active\" children, and reviewing the sharing/OWD model specifically for the skewed relationship, rather than a single silver-bullet fix. A is incorrect: OWD is an object-level setting, not something that can be overridden per individual record; there is no supported per-record OWD override. C is incorrect: A validation rule limiting save frequency doesn't address the underlying lock contention mechanism and would just frustrate legitimate business use without solving the technical root cause. D is incorrect: Alphabetical position of an Account name has no bearing on record-locking mechanics, which are driven by concurrent DML against the same parent, not naming or sort order.,")
        Question(id: "101", question: "How should file storage versus data storage limits shape the archiving plan for an LDV object where each of 50 million records has, on average, 3 attached PDF documents totaling significant space?", options: [("A", "Only the number of records matters for storage planning; the size of individual attached files has no bearing on total consumption."), ("B", "File storage automatically shrinks proportionally whenever the related object's records are archived or deleted, requiring no separate monitoring."), ("C", "PDF attachments count against data storage exclusively, with no separate file storage allocation involved at all."), ("D", "The architect should separately size and monitor file storage consumption (driven by the PDF attachments) apart from data storage (driven by the object records themselves), since archiving the records alone won't reduce file storage, and a distinct plan (e.g., external document storage integration, or coordinated deletion/archival of both record and attached files together) is needed to address both allocations.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Because file storage and data storage are tracked independently, and attached files are only removed if explicitly deleted alongside (or as part of) archiving the parent records, the architect must plan for both allocations together rather than assuming record archival alone resolves file storage growth. A is incorrect: The size of attached files directly affects total file storage consumption; ignoring file size and counting only records would badly underestimate the actual storage footprint. B is incorrect: File storage does not automatically shrink on its own; content deletion (files/attachments) must be explicitly handled as part of any archiving process, not assumed to follow record deletion automatically. C is incorrect: Attached files consume file storage, a separate allocation from data storage; this claim incorrectly merges the two distinct limits.,")
        Question(id: "102", question: "A batch Apex job processes 50 million records nightly using Database.getQueryLocator with a non-selective filter on an LDV object, occasionally hitting query timeout errors before batches even begin executing. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Switch to a stateful batch Apex class, since statefulness inherently improves the performance of the initial query locator regardless of filter design."), ("B", "Increase the batch size parameter in the batch Apex execute method, since larger batch sizes always resolve locator query timeouts regardless of filter selectivity."), ("C", "Refine the query used by the batch job's start method to use selective, indexed filter criteria (or break the job into multiple more targeted batch jobs each with a selective filter) rather than relying on a broad, non-selective query that risks timing out during the initial locator query itself."), ("D", "Move the entire job to run via Anonymous Apex in Developer Console instead of scheduled batch Apex, since Anonymous Apex is exempt from query timeout limits.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The batch job's start method query locator is still subject to normal SOQL selectivity/timeout considerations; a non-selective filter on an LDV object can time out even before any batch begins processing, so the fix is to make that locator query selective (indexed filter) or split the work into multiple targeted jobs. A is incorrect: Statefulness (via Database.Stateful) preserves instance variable state across batch executions; it has no effect on the underlying query locator's selectivity or timeout risk. B is incorrect: The execute method's batch size affects how many records are processed per batch chunk, not the performance of the initial locator query that's timing out. D is incorrect: Anonymous Apex is still subject to governor limits and query timeouts; it is not exempt from the same SOQL performance constraints as scheduled batch Apex.,")
        Question(id: "103", question: "Before migrating 5 million legacy Contact records into Salesforce, the architect discovers inconsistent phone number formats, missing required Last Name values on 8% of records, and duplicate entries under slightly different spellings. What should be prioritized before the load begins?", options: [("A", "Rely exclusively on Salesforce Duplicate Rules configured after the load to resolve all duplicate spellings automatically without any pre-load cleansing."), ("B", "Load all 5 million records as-is immediately, since Salesforce's post-load reporting tools will automatically detect and silently fix any data quality issues."), ("C", "Skip any records missing Last Name entirely during the load with no further investigation, since Contact records without Last Name have no legitimate use case."), ("D", "Perform pre-migration data profiling and cleansing (standardizing phone formats, resolving or flagging missing required fields, and running fuzzy matching to identify likely duplicates) so the load starts from a materially cleaner dataset rather than pushing these problems into Salesforce.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Identifying and addressing format inconsistencies, missing required data, and likely duplicates before the load prevents propagating bad data into Salesforce, where cleanup is generally more disruptive and expensive than pre-migration profiling and cleansing. A is incorrect: Duplicate Rules can help going forward but are not a substitute for proactively cleaning known duplicate spelling variants already identified in the legacy dataset before the load. B is incorrect: Salesforce has no automatic data quality remediation that silently fixes format inconsistencies or duplicates post-load; issues must be actively addressed, not assumed away. C is incorrect: Some \"missing Last Name\" records may represent legitimate business scenarios (e.g., organizational contacts) that deserve investigation rather than blanket exclusion without review.,")
        Question(id: "104", question: "An architect is designing an External ID strategy for a one-time migration of 2 million Orders from a legacy ERP, where future incremental updates from the same ERP are also planned. What approach best supports both the initial load and future upserts?", options: [("A", "Add an indexed, unique External ID field on the Order object storing the ERP's native order number, and use upsert (not plain insert) for both the initial load and all future incremental syncs, ensuring idempotency and avoiding duplicate creation on reruns."), ("B", "Use plain insert operations for the initial load and a completely different matching field (Order Name) for all future incremental upserts."), ("C", "Skip creating any External ID field, since Salesforce automatically generates a matching key for any object based on CreatedDate alone."), ("D", "Use External ID only for the initial load, then switch entirely to manual matching via export/reconciliation for every future incremental update.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A dedicated, indexed External ID field storing the legacy system's native key supports reliable upsert-based idempotency for both the initial bulk load and every subsequent incremental sync, avoiding duplicate creation if a load needs to be rerun or incremental updates arrive. B is incorrect: Switching matching fields between the initial load and ongoing syncs (using Order Name, which isn't guaranteed unique or stable) introduces matching risk and defeats the purpose of a consistent External ID strategy. C is incorrect: Salesforce does not generate any implicit external matching key based on CreatedDate; a genuine External ID field must be explicitly created and populated. D is incorrect: Manual reconciliation for every future incremental update at scale is unsustainable and error-prone compared to automated upsert against a stable External ID.,")
        Question(id: "105", question: "When loading Accounts, then Contacts, then Opportunities via Bulk API in a phased migration, what technique ensures referential integrity across the three related loads?", options: [("A", "Load Opportunities first, then Contacts, then Accounts, since referential integrity is only relevant when child objects are loaded before their parents."), ("B", "Load all three objects simultaneously in a single Bulk API job without any sequencing, since Salesforce automatically resolves cross-object relationships based on matching field values without any explicit reference."), ("C", "Capture each newly created Salesforce record ID (or use External ID-based upsert) from the Account load, then use those IDs (or the corresponding External ID references) to populate the AccountId lookup on the Contact load, and similarly reference Contacts/Accounts when loading Opportunities, ensuring each phase completes and is validated before the next begins."), ("D", "Skip populating AccountId on Contact records entirely during the load, planning to backfill all relationships in a single mass update after all three loads finish independently.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Sequencing the load (parent before child) and explicitly carrying forward the parent's resulting ID (via captured Salesforce IDs or External ID-based upsert) into the child's lookup field is exactly how referential integrity is maintained across phased Bulk API loads. A is incorrect: Loading child objects before their parents (Opportunities before Accounts/Contacts) would leave lookups unresolvable at load time, worsening rather than avoiding referential integrity issues. B is incorrect: Salesforce does not automatically resolve unspecified cross-object relationships in a single simultaneous load; explicit foreign key values (or External ID references) must be provided for each lookup. D is incorrect: Leaving AccountId blank and attempting a later mass backfill adds significant reconciliation complexity and risk compared to populating the relationship correctly during the sequenced load itself.,")
        Question(id: "106", question: "A large-scale initial data load of 10 million Contact records is expected to take significantly longer than anticipated because Duplicate Rules are evaluating every record against the full existing dataset. What should the architect recommend for this one-time load specifically?", options: [("A", "Convert all Duplicate Rules to Matching Rules only, since Matching Rules alone have no performance impact on any Bulk API load regardless of volume."), ("B", "Leave Duplicate Rules fully active throughout the entire load regardless of performance impact, since deactivating them even temporarily is never an acceptable practice under any circumstances."), ("C", "Permanently delete all Duplicate Rules from the org so this performance issue can never recur on any future load."), ("D", "Temporarily deactivate or adjust the relevant Duplicate Rules for the duration of the initial bulk load (documenting the change and reactivating them immediately afterward), then run a separate, deliberate deduplication pass using Data Loader/DemandTools-style matching or Salesforce's own duplicate jobs after the load completes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Temporarily adjusting Duplicate Rules specifically for the one-time high-volume load (with a documented, deliberate post-load dedupe pass) is a common, pragmatic pattern balancing load performance against maintaining eventual data quality, rather than permanently sacrificing either. A is incorrect: Matching Rules still get evaluated as part of Duplicate Rule processing during a load; converting doesn't eliminate the underlying evaluation overhead during Bulk API operations. B is incorrect: Leaving Duplicate Rules fully active despite a known severe performance impact ignores a legitimate, well-established mitigation used specifically for one-time bulk loads. C is incorrect: Permanently removing Duplicate Rules eliminates ongoing duplicate prevention for all future day-to-day record creation, which is a much bigger loss than the temporary load-time adjustment needed.,")
        Question(id: "107", question: "Legacy data being migrated has records missing values in fields that are marked as required in Salesforce, and the business has accepted this as a known, temporary gap to be resolved in a later cleanup phase. Which statements describe appropriate governance for bypassing validation to allow this data through? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "There is no governance risk at all, since any required field restriction only applies to UI-based record creation and never to migrated records."), ("B", "The only acceptable approach is to abandon the migration entirely rather than ever load a record with a missing required value."), ("C", "Bypassing validation rules for migration automatically triggers Salesforce to schedule a remediation job with zero configuration."), ("D", "Bypassing validation (e.g., via a migration-only permission set or temporarily relaxing the rule) introduces genuinely incomplete records into production."), ("E", "The architect should require a tracked remediation plan (e.g., a flag field and a scheduled follow-up cleanup job/report) rather than silently leaving gaps unaddressed indefinitely.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "Deliberately allowing incomplete records through (D) is sometimes a legitimate trade-off, but it must come with an explicit tracking and remediation plan (E) so the temporary gap doesn't silently become permanent, which is the real governance risk to manage. A is incorrect: Required field validation, when enforced via validation rules, applies across UI, API, and Bulk API/Data Loader alike; claiming it only applies to UI entry misrepresents how the enforcement mechanism actually works. B is incorrect: Abandoning the migration entirely is an extreme, usually impractical response; a tracked, deliberate exception with a remediation plan is the standard pragmatic approach instead. C is incorrect: There is no automatic remediation scheduling triggered simply by bypassing validation; any follow-up cleanup process must be deliberately designed and implemented.,")
        Question(id: "108", question: "A data reconciliation process after a large migration compares source and target record counts and samples field values for accuracy. What additional reconciliation technique specifically helps detect subtle data corruption (e.g., truncated text, encoding issues) that simple record counts would miss?", options: [("A", "Manually opening five arbitrary records in the UI and visually confirming they look reasonable, without any systematic sampling methodology."), ("B", "Comparing only the total row count between source and target, since row count alone is fully sufficient to guarantee content-level data integrity."), ("C", "Verifying that the migration job completed with a \"Success\" status in the migration tool's log, which alone guarantees byte-for-byte content accuracy for every field."), ("D", "Checksum or hash comparison of key field values (or full-record hashes) between source and migrated target records, since this can detect content-level discrepancies that a matching record count alone would not reveal.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Checksum/hash comparison directly detects content-level differences (like truncation or encoding corruption) between source and target values that a pure count-based check cannot catch, making it a valuable complement to reconciliation reviews focused only on volume. A is incorrect: An unsystematic, small manual sample lacks the statistical rigor to catch subtle, low-frequency corruption issues across millions of migrated records. B is incorrect: Matching row counts say nothing about whether the content within each migrated record accurately reflects the source; corruption within existing rows would go undetected. C is incorrect: A tool-reported \"Success\" status generally reflects that records were written without an error, not that field-level content is byte-for-byte accurate; data can load \"successfully\" while still being corrupted or truncated.,")
        Question(id: "109", question: "A company must copy production data containing customer PII into a new full sandbox for migration testing. What should the architect require as part of this process?", options: [("A", "Copy the production data into the sandbox with no masking at all, since sandboxes inherit identical security controls and user restrictions as production by default."), ("B", "Apply data masking (e.g., via Salesforce Data Mask or an equivalent tool) to PII fields in the sandbox after the refresh, since testing environments generally have looser access controls than production and shouldn't expose real customer PII to the broader testing team."), ("C", "Skip creating a full sandbox altogether and test the migration exclusively in production directly, since this avoids any masking requirement entirely."), ("D", "Mask only the Account Name field, since Account Name is the only field considered PII under any data protection regulation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Because sandboxes typically have broader or different access than production, masking real PII (e.g., using a tool like Data Mask) before or immediately after refreshing production data into a sandbox is a standard safeguard to prevent unnecessary exposure of sensitive customer data during testing. A is incorrect: Sandboxes do not automatically inherit identical access restrictions to production; broader internal access to test environments is exactly why masking is recommended. C is incorrect: Testing a migration directly in production is a high-risk practice that risks corrupting live data, and it doesn't address or eliminate the underlying PII exposure concern in test environments generally. D is incorrect: PII encompasses many possible fields (email, phone, SSN, address, etc.) well beyond just Account Name, so masking only one field would leave substantial exposure.,")
        Question(id: "110", question: "A delta migration strategy uses a \"LastModifiedDate\" watermark field from the legacy system to pull only changed records since the last extraction. What risk should the architect flag if the legacy system's timestamps are known to be unreliable (e.g., inconsistent time zones, occasional clock drift)?", options: [("A", "Any timestamp inconsistency in the source system is automatically corrected by Bulk API before the data is written to Salesforce."), ("B", "Watermark-based delta migration is entirely immune to source timestamp issues, since Salesforce automatically normalizes all incoming timestamp values to a single canonical time zone."), ("C", "Unreliable source timestamps can cause the delta process to miss recently changed records (if the watermark is set too late) or repeatedly reprocess unchanged records (if set too early), so the architect should build in a safety overlap window and/or a secondary validation mechanism (e.g., periodic full reconciliation) rather than trusting the watermark alone."), ("D", "Delta migrations should never use a watermark field of any kind; only full-object re-extraction on every run can be considered reliable.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Because unreliable timestamps directly threaten the correctness of a watermark-based delta approach (risking both missed and duplicate reprocessing), a defensive overlap window plus a periodic broader reconciliation check are the standard mitigations rather than trusting the raw watermark value unconditionally. A is incorrect: Bulk API loads whatever values it's given; it has no built-in mechanism to detect or fix inconsistent source timestamps before writing records. B is incorrect: Salesforce does not automatically detect or correct inconsistent source system time zone handling; any normalization must be handled by the integration/migration logic itself. D is incorrect: Full-object re-extraction on every run is often impractical at scale; a well-designed watermark approach with safety overlap is generally the more scalable choice, not a practice to avoid entirely.,")
        Question(id: "111", question: "What are the primary performance trade-offs between Bulk API 1.0 and Bulk API 2.0 that an architect should communicate when planning a 20-million-record migration?", options: [("A", "Bulk API 1.0 and 2.0 are functionally and operationally identical in every respect, with the version number reflecting only a marketing change with no technical differences."), ("B", "Bulk API 2.0 simplifies job management by automatically chunking data into batches without requiring the client to manually manage batch sizing and job/batch state, whereas Bulk API 1.0 gives more granular manual control over batch composition and sizing, which some architects still prefer for finely tuned performance tuning scenarios."), ("C", "Bulk API 2.0 requires every batch to be exactly 1 record in size, making it strictly slower than Bulk API 1.0 for any large migration."), ("D", "Bulk API 1.0 has no concept of batches at all, processing the entire 20 million records as a single atomic transaction.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Bulk API 2.0's automatic batch chunking simplifies client-side implementation and job management, while Bulk API 1.0's manual batch control appeals to teams wanting fine-grained tuning over batch composition and size for specific performance characteristics, which is the genuine trade-off to communicate. A is incorrect: The two versions differ meaningfully in how batching is managed (automatic vs. manual), which is a real operational and architectural distinction, not just branding. C is incorrect: Bulk API 2.0 does not force a fixed batch size of 1; it automatically manages chunking at a reasonable size, and this claim about it being strictly slower is incorrect. D is incorrect: Bulk API 1.0 explicitly uses batches (client-defined) within a job; it does not process everything as a single atomic transaction.,")
        Question(id: "112", question: "How does the choice of Bulk API batch size affect both load performance and error granularity during a large migration, and what should the architect recommend as a starting approach?", options: [("A", "Batch size has no measurable effect on either performance or error granularity; it is purely a cosmetic setting with no functional consequence."), ("B", "Smaller batch sizes provide finer error granularity (isolating failures to a smaller set of records) but can increase per-batch overhead, while larger batch sizes improve overall throughput but make it harder to isolate exactly which records failed within a batch, so the architect should recommend testing a few batch sizes against a representative data sample to find the right balance for this specific migration's data shape and automation footprint."), ("C", "The largest possible batch size always minimizes both processing time and error investigation effort simultaneously, with no trade-off to consider."), ("D", "Batch size should always be set to the theoretical maximum record count for the object's total volume in a single batch, since Bulk API removes the standard 10,000-record-per-batch guidance for any use case.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "This captures the genuine trade-off: smaller batches ease error isolation and investigation but add relative overhead, larger batches boost throughput but complicate pinpointing failed records, and testing against representative sample data is the standard way to tune batch size for the migration's actual characteristics. A is incorrect: Batch size does materially affect both throughput and how easily failed records can be isolated and investigated; treating it as purely cosmetic ignores real operational trade-offs. C is incorrect: Maximizing batch size can actually hurt error granularity investigation and, in some cases with heavy automation, contribute to governor limit pressure, so it isn't a strictly beneficial choice with no downside. D is incorrect: Standard Bulk API batch size guidance (commonly up to 10,000 records per batch, depending on API version and context) still applies and shouldn't be ignored by assuming there's no practical batching guidance at all.,")
        Question(id: "113", question: "When migrating Opportunities with lookups to Accounts and Contacts, several records fail due to unresolved foreign keys because the referenced parent records hadn't yet been created at the time of load. Which practices minimize this failure pattern? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Retry failed Opportunity records indefinitely without changing load order, since eventually all parent records will exist and resolve the lookups automatically through repeated retries alone."), ("B", "Establish and validate a complete parent-to-child load order (Accounts, then Contacts, then Opportunities) before the dependent object's batch begins."), ("C", "Load all objects in a single combined file sorted alphabetically by object API name, since alphabetical ordering guarantees correct parent-child resolution automatically."), ("D", "Populate Opportunity lookups with placeholder text values instead of actual IDs, planning to replace them with real IDs in an unspecified future step."), ("E", "Build an ID mapping table (legacy key to new Salesforce ID, or use External ID-based upsert) that is fully populated and confirmed before loading the dependent Opportunity batch.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Ensuring parent objects are fully loaded in the correct order (B) and that their IDs are captured or reliably resolvable via a validated mapping (E) before the dependent child batch begins is the direct fix for unresolved foreign key failures caused by out-of-order or concurrent loading. A is incorrect: Blind indefinite retries without addressing the root ordering issue waste processing time and don't guarantee correctness if the mapping between legacy and new IDs was never properly established. C is incorrect: Alphabetical sorting of object API names has no logical connection to actual parent-child dependency order and would not reliably prevent unresolved foreign key errors. D is incorrect: Placeholder text values in a lookup field are not valid IDs and would themselves cause load failures or corrupt data; this isn't a workable interim strategy.,")
        Question(id: "114", question: "What data consistency risk does disabling triggers, workflow rules, and Process Builder/Flow automation during a bulk data load introduce, and how should the architect address it?", options: [("A", "Disabling automation means data that would normally be derived, defaulted, or validated by that automation (e.g., calculated fields, cascading updates) won't be applied during the load, so the architect should plan a post-load remediation step (e.g., a batch Apex job or scheduled Flow) to run the equivalent logic once automation is safely re-enabled."), ("B", "Disabling automation has zero consistency impact, since Salesforce automatically applies all trigger and Flow logic retroactively once automation is re-enabled."), ("C", "Automation should never be disabled for any bulk load, regardless of governor limit or performance risk, since there is no valid business or technical reason to do so."), ("D", "Once automation is disabled for a load, it can never be safely re-enabled afterward without a full data reload from scratch.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Skipping automation during a load intentionally improves load performance but means dependent logic (defaults, derived values, cascading effects) won't run automatically, so a deliberate post-load remediation pass is needed to bring the data in line with what normal automation would have produced. B is incorrect: Salesforce does not retroactively apply skipped trigger/Flow logic once automation is turned back on; any gap must be explicitly remediated by a dedicated process. C is incorrect: Disabling automation for performance during a well-planned bulk load is a common, valid technique when properly paired with a remediation plan; blanket prohibition ignores legitimate architectural trade-offs. D is incorrect: Automation can be safely re-enabled after a load without requiring a full data reload; it simply resumes applying to new/future transactions going forward, with any historical gap addressed via remediation.,")
        Question(id: "115", question: "What is the impact of index strategy sequencing (disabling versus keeping custom indexes active) during a very large initial data load, and what should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Indexes must always be fully rebuilt from scratch before any load can begin, regardless of data volume, adding a mandatory fixed delay to every migration."), ("B", "Index strategy has no bearing whatsoever on load performance; indexes are irrelevant during data loading and only matter for later reporting."), ("C", "All custom indexes are automatically and permanently disabled the moment any Bulk API job begins, requiring no explicit planning by the architect."), ("D", "Depending on the volume and specific indexes involved, it may be more efficient to load data first and then have indexes (re)built or validated afterward rather than maintaining full index upkeep during every single insert, but this trade-off should be evaluated with Salesforce Support guidance for genuinely massive LDV-scale loads rather than assumed universally.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "For genuinely massive loads, index maintenance overhead during each insert can be a meaningful cost, so evaluating (with Salesforce Support where custom/skinny-table indexes are involved) whether to sequence index activity around the load is a legitimate architectural consideration rather than a one-size-fits-all rule. A is incorrect: Indexes do not need to be rebuilt from scratch before every load regardless of volume; this is only a meaningful consideration for very large, specific scenarios, not a universal mandatory step. B is incorrect: Index maintenance does add overhead during inserts at large volume; it is not irrelevant purely to reporting, making this an incorrect blanket dismissal. C is incorrect: There is no platform behavior that automatically and permanently disables custom indexes simply because a Bulk API job starts; any such change requires deliberate action.,")
        Question(id: "116", question: "How does sharing recalculation specifically affect data load performance for an object with Private OWD during a migration of 15 million records, and what technique helps mitigate this?", options: [("A", "There is no way to influence sharing recalculation timing in Salesforce; it always runs synchronously and identically regardless of OWD or data volume."), ("B", "Sharing recalculation only occurs for objects with Public Read/Write OWD, so Private OWD objects are entirely unaffected by this concern during a load."), ("C", "Sharing recalculation is a one-time, permanent event that happens only once during the entire lifetime of an org and is unrelated to any specific data load."), ("D", "Every insert into a Private-OWD object with active sharing rules can trigger sharing recalculation, and at high volume this adds substantial overhead, so techniques like deferring sharing calculations during the load (using the deferred sharing calculation capability, where available) and manually triggering a single recalculation after the load completes can significantly improve load performance.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Private OWD combined with sharing rules means inserts can trigger recalculation work, which compounds at high volume; deferring recalculation during the bulk load and triggering it once at the end is a recognized mitigation for exactly this kind of large migration performance concern. A is incorrect: Salesforce does offer mechanisms (like deferred sharing calculation) to influence when recalculation occurs, particularly relevant for large-scale operations like migrations; claiming no control exists is inaccurate. B is incorrect: Sharing recalculation concerns are actually more relevant for Private OWD objects with sharing rules, not Public Read/Write ones, which generally require far less sharing rule-driven recalculation. C is incorrect: Sharing recalculation is triggered by relevant events (like ownership changes, new sharing rules, or bulk inserts under certain conditions) throughout an org's life, not a single one-time event tied only to org creation.,")
        Question(id: "117", question: "A phased cutover plan is being designed for migrating from a legacy CRM to Salesforce, including a full go-live weekend. What should the architect include specifically to address the risk of a failed cutover?", options: [("A", "A plan that skips any validation step entirely, since post-migration validation delays the go-live timeline unnecessarily."), ("B", "A cutover plan that immediately decommissions the legacy system's database the moment the migration job reports \"Success,\" eliminating any need for a rollback contingency."), ("C", "A defined rollback strategy (e.g., keeping the legacy system as the system of record and fully operational until Salesforce data is validated, with a clear go/no-go checkpoint and criteria for reverting users back to the legacy system if validation fails) rather than an irreversible one-way cutover with no fallback."), ("D", "A cutover plan requiring users to manually re-enter all data directly into Salesforce during the go-live weekend as a validation mechanism, bypassing the migrated data entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A defined go/no-go checkpoint with explicit rollback criteria, paired with keeping the legacy system available and authoritative until validation passes, is the standard way to manage cutover risk and avoid being stuck with a failed migration and no fallback path. A is incorrect: Skipping validation entirely increases the risk of discovering critical data issues only after users are already relying on Salesforce, which is precisely the scenario a rollback plan is meant to guard against. B is incorrect: Immediately decommissioning the legacy system based solely on a load-tool \"Success\" status removes any ability to roll back if post-load validation later reveals problems. D is incorrect: Manual re-entry during the go-live weekend defeats the purpose of the migration itself and is not a scalable or realistic validation technique for a large cutover.,")
        Question(id: "118", question: "What are the primary differences between the Bulk API Query operation and REST API SOQL queries when exporting 50 million records from Salesforce, and when should each be used?", options: [("A", "Bulk API Query can only export data in JSON format, while REST API SOQL queries can only export data in CSV format, making the choice purely about desired file format."), ("B", "REST API SOQL queries have no row limit whatsoever and are always the superior choice for exporting any volume of data, including tens of millions of records."), ("C", "Bulk API Query and REST API SOQL queries are functionally identical, with the only difference being which HTTP verb is used to invoke them."), ("D", "Bulk API Query is optimized for large asynchronous exports, processing and returning results in batches suited to high record volumes, while REST API SOQL queries (even with queryMore/nextRecordsUrl pagination) are better suited to smaller, more interactive query needs and can be less efficient at very high volumes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Bulk API Query is specifically built for large-scale asynchronous data extraction, handling high volumes more efficiently than the more interactive, synchronous-oriented REST API SOQL query mechanism, which is why it's generally preferred for very large exports like this scenario's 50 million records. A is incorrect: Both mechanisms support multiple output format options in practice depending on tooling; the format is not a rigid one-to-one constraint tied exclusively to which API is used. B is incorrect: REST API SOQL queries are subject to pagination and are generally less efficient for very large-scale bulk extraction compared to the purpose-built Bulk API Query operation; this claim overstates REST's suitability at this volume. C is incorrect: The two mechanisms differ meaningfully in execution model (synchronous/paginated vs. asynchronous batch-oriented), not merely in HTTP verb; treating them as identical ignores this key architectural distinction.,")
        Question(id: "119", question: "What consideration should an architect raise when exporting ContentVersion (file) data alongside standard object records as part of a full data export strategy, especially regarding binary content and API limits?", options: [("A", "File exports are exempt from all Salesforce API limits regardless of file size or volume, since binary content is treated as unmetered by definition."), ("B", "ContentVersion binary content is automatically included as an additional column in any standard Bulk API Query export of object records with no special handling required."), ("C", "Because file binary content (VersionData) must be retrieved through dedicated calls rather than as part of a standard object field export, and because binary payloads consume response size differently than plain field data, the export design should account for this separately — potentially retrieving files in a distinct, paced process rather than assuming they come along automatically with the object record export."), ("D", "There is no meaningful difference between exporting field data and exporting file binary content; both are retrieved identically in a single unified call.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Binary file content requires a distinct retrieval path (via ContentVersion's VersionData) separate from standard field-level export, and its different size/limit characteristics mean the export plan needs its own paced, dedicated handling rather than assuming it rides along with a standard object export. A is incorrect: File/binary retrieval is still subject to relevant API limits (such as response size and API call limits); there is no blanket exemption for binary content. B is incorrect: Standard Bulk API Query exports of object fields do not automatically include binary file content as a column; a separate retrieval mechanism is required for that content. D is incorrect: Field data and binary file content have materially different retrieval mechanisms and constraints; treating them as identical ignores a key export design consideration.,")
        Question(id: "120", question: "A financial services company must export data from an org protected by Shield Platform Encryption, field-level security restricting several fields to specific profiles, and object-level sharing rules, for a one-time regulatory audit. What governance consideration is most critical when planning this export?", options: [("A", "Any System Administrator profile automatically bypasses field-level security, sharing rules, and encryption visibility restrictions uniformly across every Salesforce org, making user provisioning irrelevant to the export outcome."), ("B", "The permissions of the specific user account performing the export (their profile's field-level security, \"View Encrypted Data\" permission, and record-level sharing access) directly determine what data actually appears in the exported file, so the export user must be deliberately provisioned with exactly the access needed for the audit, and that access should be reviewed/removed afterward."), ("C", "Exports for regulatory audits are automatically exempt from field-level security and sharing rule enforcement, since audit purposes override standard platform access controls."), ("D", "Shield Platform Encryption, field-level security, and sharing rules apply only to UI-based access and have no bearing whatsoever on data retrieved through export tooling like Data Loader or the API.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Because export output is governed by the exporting user's actual permissions (field-level security, View Encrypted Data, and sharing-based record access), the architect must deliberately provision (and later de-provision) an export user with precisely the access needed for the audit, rather than assuming any generic elevated access is either sufficient or safely scoped. A is incorrect: A System Administrator profile can be configured with broad access, but that is a permission-set/profile design choice, not a platform guarantee; encryption visibility (View Encrypted Data) and sharing are still governed by the specific grants assigned, so assuming any Admin automatically sees everything is not something the architect should rely on for a controlled audit export. C is incorrect: There is no platform-level exemption that lifts field-level security or sharing enforcement simply because the purpose is a regulatory audit; access must still be explicitly and deliberately granted to the exporting user. D is incorrect: Field-level security, sharing rules, and Shield encryption visibility all apply equally to API-based and Data Loader access, not just the UI; export results are shaped by exactly the same permission model as interactive access.")
